import{i as e,r as t}from"./scores-PQYd482h.js";var n=[{n:1,unit:`voice`,short:`Hello`,title:`Hello, Music Class`,focus:`Learn how music class works: we sing, we listen, we move, and we stop when the hand goes up.`,outcome:`Students enter a music class, echo a greeting, and freeze on a silent conductor cue.`,materials:[`Open space or chairs in a circle`,`This page on the projector`,`A small drum or clap`],warmup:{title:`Name wave`,detail:`Teacher sings “Hello, _____” on so–mi. The class echoes. Go around as many names as five minutes allow. If time is short, greet the whole class: “Hello, Grade One.”`},teach:[{title:`How we do music`,detail:`In this room we sing, we play, we move, and we listen. We take turns with our voices. We keep our bodies in our own space.`},{title:`The quiet hand`,detail:`When the teacher’s hand goes up, voices and instruments rest. Practice three times: talk, hand up, freeze. Smile when they freeze. Do not shout over them.`},{title:`A hello song`,detail:`Teach “Hello Everybody” (or “Hello, Hello”) by echo. Phrase one, class copies. Phrase two, class copies. Then the whole thing. Keep it high enough for children’s voices — not an adult low voice.`},{title:`Sit like a singer`,detail:`Feet on the floor, tall backs, hands in laps. “If your chair is a drum, we are not playing it yet.”`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`The Listening Walk`,author:`Paul Showers (illus. Aliki)`,why:`Models quiet listening, stopping to notice sounds, and sing–listen–move–stop classroom routines.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Listening-Walk-Paul-Showers/dp/0064433226`,guide:{"when": "Hook for the Teach block, right after the Name wave (about 7 min). Stop at the park if time is short and finish next week.", "before": ["“The girl in this book does not talk on her walk. Why might that help her hear more?”", "Practise the quiet hand once: hand up, freeze, listen for five seconds. “What did you hear?”"], "during": ["Echo each sound word the way the book says it (Major’s toenails on the sidewalk, Dad’s shoes, the lawnmower, the sprinkler). Keep the echo the same size as your model.", "After every two or three sounds, raise the quiet hand. Everyone freezes. Smile, then turn the page.", "At the jackhammer, cover ears: that is a loud sound. In the park, whisper the soft sounds (pigeons, ducks, the woodpecker tapping, crickets, wind in the leaves).", "Tap the woodpecker on your knee as a four-beat pattern. Class echoes."], "after": "Do our own listening walk sitting down: eyes closed for 20 seconds, then quiet hand. Two or three children name one sound. Then sing the hello song once more, standing.", "check": "“What does the girl do so she can hear more?” “What does our quiet hand ask us to do?”", "adapt": "English learners: show quick pictures (dog, shoe, lawnmower, duck, jet) and point as you read. Wigglers: tiptoe in place during the park pages.", "extend": "Take a two-minute hallway listening walk. Back in class, children draw one sound they heard."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kzQOdEAIH5E", "channel": "KidTimeStoryTime"}},activity:{title:`Echo the teacher`,detail:`Teacher claps a 4-beat pattern. Class claps it back. Then teacher sings a 4-beat so–mi snippet on “loo.” Class sings it back. End with the hello song once more, standing.`},close:`Ask: “What does the quiet hand mean?” Stamp the lesson when most of the class can freeze without a reminder.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sit in your music spot`,detail:`Find the spot your teacher shows. Hands in your lap. Eyes on the teacher.`},{title:`Sing hello`,detail:`When the teacher sings hello to you, sing hello back with your singing voice.`},{title:`Watch the hand`,detail:`When the hand goes up, your voice stops. That is how we rest together.`},{title:`Echo a clap`,detail:`Copy the teacher’s claps. Same number, same speed.`},{title:`Echo a tune`,detail:`Copy the teacher’s “loo.” Same high and low.`}],teacher:[`Grade 1 is six. Keep instructions to one sentence, then do it.`,`If a child will not sing yet, let them mouth the words. Do not force a solo on week 1.`,`Stand where every child can see your hand. The quiet hand only works if they can see it.`,`Names matter. Misheard names break trust on day one — ask once if you are unsure.`],checklist:[`I know my music spot.`,`I can freeze when the hand goes up.`,`I sang hello with the class.`],mistakes:[`Starting with a long talk. Sing first.`,`Using a low adult key. Children need a higher starting pitch.`,`Praising volume. We want a singing voice, not a shout.`],challenge:`Be the leader: clap a tiny pattern and let the class echo you once.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw yourself in music class. Circle the quiet hand.`,items:[{id:`draw`,kind:`text`,label:`I look like this in music class (words or a little picture note):`,lines:3},{id:`hand`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the teacher’s hand goes up I`,choices:[`keep singing`,`freeze and rest`,`get louder`]}]},faith:`Optional: we use our voices as a gift. We take turns so every voice has room.`},{n:2,unit:`voice`,short:`Two voices`,title:`Talking Voice, Singing Voice`,focus:`Feel the difference between a speaking voice and a singing voice, and find a light singing tone.`,outcome:`Students distinguish children’s speaking and singing voices and use a singing voice in echo.`,materials:[`Board: Talk / Sing`,`A picture book sentence`,`This page`],warmup:{title:`Four voices`,detail:`Whisper “good morning,” speak it, call it (playground voice), then sing it on so–mi. Students copy each one. Name them: whisper, talk, call, sing.`},teach:[{title:`Talking sits, singing floats`,detail:`Say “hello” in a talking voice. Now sing “hello” on so–mi. Put a hand on your head for sing, on your chest for talk, so students have a body cue.`},{title:`We do not shout songs`,detail:`A shout hurts ears and does not stay in tune. If the class gets loud, drop to a whisper-sing and they will follow.`},{title:`Match my pitch`,detail:`Sing so on “loo.” Class matches. Then mi. Then so–mi. Smile and nod for close matches. Do not single out a child who is low — sing beside them later.`},{title:`Song: Rain, Rain`,detail:`“Rain, rain, go away” is so–mi. Echo by phrase. Add a gentle finger rain. Keep it light.`}],story:{title:`Mina’s two voices`,body:`Mina used her talking voice to tell Sam about her new boots. Then Mrs. Cole sang “hello.” Mina tried to sing, but it came out like a yell. Mrs. Cole whispered the tune. Mina whispered it back. The whisper grew into a small, floating singing voice. Sam heard it and smiled. Two voices: one for words, one for songs.`},storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Mortimer`,author:`Robert Munsch (illus. Michael Martchenko)`,why:`Spoken family dialogue contrasts with Mortimer’s sung refrain (“Clang, clang, rattle-bing-bang”).`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Mortimer-Robert-Munsch/dp/0920303110`,guide:{"when": "Mid-lesson, after Match my pitch and before the Sort the voice activity (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Listen for two kinds of voices: people talking and Mortimer singing.”", "Set the body cues: hand on chest for talk, hand on head for sing."], "during": ["Every time Mortimer sings “Clang, clang, rattle-bing-bang, gonna make my noise all day,” the class sings it with you on a light so–mi tune, hand on head.", "When his mother, his father, the 17 brothers and sisters, and then the two police officers tell him “Mortimer, be quiet,” say it in a talking voice, hand on chest. Try the police line in a low, slow talking voice.", "Each time someone climbs the stairs, pat a slow, steady beat on laps.", "Point out that the family gets louder and louder, but a shout is not a song. Keep Mortimer’s song light every time. Whisper-sing it at the end when he falls asleep."], "after": "Play Sort the voice with lines from the book. Teacher says “Mortimer, be quiet!” or sings “Clang, clang…”. Students show chest or head. Finish with Rain, Rain.", "check": "“When Mortimer made his noise, was it a talking voice or a singing voice? How do you know?”", "adapt": "Hold up a TALK card and a SING card. The refrain repeats, so new English speakers can join by the second time.", "extend": "Invent a new “noise line” for Mortimer and sing it on so–mi. Class echoes."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ixClFSWjgQ", "channel": "Robert Munsch (official audio)", "label": "Listen: Robert Munsch reads it"}},activity:{title:`Sort the voice`,detail:`Teacher says or sings a line. Students show talk (hand on chest) or sing (hand on head). Mix them. End by singing Rain, Rain all the way through.`},close:`Ask two students to demonstrate talk, then sing, on the word “hello.” Stamp when most hands sort correctly.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Copy four voices`,detail:`Whisper, talk, call, sing. Do the one the teacher shows.`},{title:`Hand on chest or head`,detail:`Chest = talking. Head = singing.`},{title:`Match “loo”`,detail:`Sing the same high or low as the teacher. Soft is okay.`},{title:`Sing Rain, Rain`,detail:`Use your singing voice. Finger rain is quiet.`},{title:`Sort talk or sing`,detail:`Listen. Show the right hand sign.`}],teacher:[`Calling voice (playground) is useful to name so they do not use it for songs.`,`If a child only speaks the song, they are still in. Invite, do not force.`,`Pitch-match in a small group during the activity if one child is lost.`],checklist:[`I can show talking voice and singing voice.`,`I did not shout the song.`,`I sang Rain, Rain with the class.`],mistakes:[`Praising the loudest singer.`,`Skipping the body cue. Grade 1 needs something to hold.`],challenge:`Sing your name on so–mi. The class echoes it.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Mark talking or singing.`,items:[{id:`v1`,kind:`choice`,label:`“Please pass the crayons.”`,choices:[`talking`,`singing`]},{id:`v2`,kind:`choice`,label:`“Rain, rain, go away” (the tune)`,choices:[`talking`,`singing`]},{id:`v3`,kind:`choice`,label:`A good music singing voice is`,choices:[`a shout`,`light and floating`,`a whisper only`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:3,unit:`voice`,short:`Echo`,title:`Echo Me`,focus:`Copy short claps and short sung patterns. Echo is how Grade 1 learns almost every song.`,outcome:`Students respond to tone matching and echo games with voice and body.`,materials:[`Drum or lap`,`So–mi ready in your head`,`Space to stand`],warmup:{title:`Copy cat claps`,detail:`Four-beat claps, class echoes. Start easy (ta ta ta ta). Add one rest. Keep a smile. If they rush, slow your model, do not say “you’re rushing.”`},teach:[{title:`Echo means copy`,detail:`I go. You go. Same speed, same number, same tune. We do not add extra claps.`},{title:`Voice echo`,detail:`Sing so–mi on “yoo.” Class echoes. Change the last note sometimes. They have to listen, not guess.`},{title:`Engine, Engine`,detail:`Chant “Engine, engine, number nine” in a speaking voice with a steady beat on laps. Then sing a so–mi version of the first line. Echo by two bars.`},{title:`Alone and together`,detail:`Once the class is strong, let one brave child be the leader for a clap echo. Sitting is fine. No one has to stand in front.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`From Head to Toe`,author:`Eric Carle`,why:`Call-and-echo structure (“Can you do it? I can do it!”) supports echoing claps and sung patterns.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Head-Toe-Eric-Carle/dp/0140563784`,guide:{"when": "Brain break between Teach and Leader for a turn, standing in own space (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Every page asks a question: ‘Can you do it?’ Listen for the answer.”", "“An echo copies exactly: same moves, same number, same speed.”"], "during": ["Read each animal line and model the move four times on a steady beat: penguin turns head, giraffe bends neck, buffalo raises shoulders, monkey waves arms, seal claps hands, gorilla thumps chest (gently), cat arches back, crocodile wiggles hips, camel bends knees, donkey kicks legs, elephant stomps foot.", "Class answers “I can do it!” on so–mi, then echoes the move exactly four times. No extra moves.", "On the seal page, clap a four-beat pattern and have the class echo it. On the elephant page, stomp softly: small feet, big listening."], "after": "Sing “Can you do it?” on so–mi with a four-beat clap pattern. Class sings “I can do it!” and echoes it. Then let two or three students lead a pattern, just like the Leader for a turn activity.", "check": "“Did your echo have the same number of claps as mine, or did you add extra?”", "adapt": "Offer a seated version of every move. English learners: touch and name each body part as you read.", "extend": "Pairs invent a new animal move and teach it to the class as a four-beat echo."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pzc4lGh19G8", "channel": "The World of Eric Carle"}},activity:{title:`Leader for a turn`,detail:`Circle. Teacher leads three echoes. Then three students each lead one clap pattern. Class copies. Finish with Engine, Engine all together.`},close:`Ask: “Does an echo add extra sounds?” No. Stamp when echoes stay the same length as the model.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Wait for the whole pattern`,detail:`Do not start copying until the teacher is done.`},{title:`Copy claps`,detail:`Same number. Same speed.`},{title:`Copy the sung “yoo”`,detail:`Same high and low.`},{title:`Chant Engine, Engine`,detail:`Pat the beat on your legs while you say the words.`},{title:`Lead one clap if you want`,detail:`Four beats. The class will copy you.`}],teacher:[`Wait. The pause before they echo is where listening lives.`,`If they start early, freeze, smile, try again. Do not scold.`,`Keep sung echoes in so–mi this week. La can wait.`],checklist:[`I waited until the teacher finished.`,`My echo was the same length.`,`I chanted Engine, Engine with the beat.`],mistakes:[`Patterns that are too long. Four beats is enough.`,`Talking through their echo. Model, then silence.`],challenge:`Sing an echo on “yoo” that goes so–mi–so–mi. Can a friend copy it?`,worksheet:{prompt:`What is an echo?`,items:[{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`An echo is`,choices:[`copy the teacher`,`make up a new song`,`get louder`]},{id:`wait`,kind:`choice`,label:`I start my echo`,choices:[`in the middle`,`when the teacher is done`,`whenever I want`]}]}},{n:4,unit:`voice`,short:`So–mi`,title:`So and Mi`,focus:`Two pitches: so (higher) and mi (lower). Hand signs and a tiny song.`,outcome:`Students respond to so–mi hand signals and sing so–mi songs in tune.`,materials:[`Space for hands`,`See-Saw or Rain, Rain`,`Board: so (high) / mi (low)`],warmup:{title:`Elevator`,detail:`Hands low: hum a low mi. Hands high: hum a higher so. Ride the elevator three times. No words yet.`},teach:[{title:`So is higher, mi is lower`,detail:`Show Kodály so (palm forward at forehead) and mi (palm in at chin). Students copy the signs without sound, then with “so” and “mi.”`},{title:`I sing, you sign`,detail:`Teacher sings so or mi. Students only show the hand sign. Then reverse: teacher signs, students sing.`},{title:`See-Saw`,detail:`“See-saw, up and down” sits on so–mi. Bounce a gentle knee bend on the beat. Echo, then all.`},{title:`Do not stretch so too high`,detail:`If so is a strain, drop the whole song a bit. Grade 1 should not look tight in the neck.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Rain, Rain, Go Away! (Sing and Read Storybook)`,author:`illustrated by Viviana Garofoli`,why:`Nursery song commonly taught as a classic so–mi piece for Grade 1.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Rain-Away-Sing-Read/dp/0545463637`,guide:{"when": "Close (5 min). The book is the song, so sing it instead of reading it.", "before": ["“This whole book is a song. Listen for the words ‘rain, rain’. Which word is higher?”", "Review the signs: so at the forehead, mi at the chin."], "during": ["Sing each page on the Rain, Rain tune. Students show so–mi hand signs on “rain, rain” every time it comes back.", "Use the changing verses to keep interest: the boys and girls who want to play, flowers that bloom in May, coming again on Saturday, the animals, and the sun that wants to stay.", "Add gentle finger rain on laps during the rainy pages. When the sun comes out at the end, hands open wide and rest."], "after": "Sing one verse again. Choose one child to be the “hand teacher” while the class sings.", "check": "Sing “rain, rain” and ask: “Show me the sign for the higher note.” (So.)", "adapt": "Children who are not ready to sign can point up for so and down for mi. Everyone can sing just “rain, rain” if the full verse is too much.", "extend": "Make up a new verse with a class name or a weekday (“come again on Monday”)."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LjeW3xOHiPg", "channel": "The Bookworms"}},activity:{title:`Sign the song`,detail:`Sing See-Saw. Students show so and mi while they sing. Then a few students take turns being the “hand teacher” while the class sings.`},close:`Silent hand signs: class sings what they see. Two patterns. Stamp when most signs match the sound.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show so`,detail:`Hand up near your forehead. That is the higher one.`},{title:`Show mi`,detail:`Hand a little lower. That is the lower one.`},{title:`Sing what the hands say`,detail:`Watch the teacher’s hands. Sing so or mi.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw`,detail:`Bend your knees a little on the beat. Use a singing voice.`},{title:`Sign while you sing`,detail:`Hands and voice do the same job.`}],teacher:[`Hand signs stay in front of the body, not wildly over the head.`,`If a child reverses so and mi, stand beside them and do it together. Do not quiz them in front of the class.`,`See-Saw, Rain Rain, and Star Light are enough repertoire this week.`],checklist:[`I can show so and mi with my hands.`,`I know so is higher.`,`I sang See-Saw with the class.`],mistakes:[`Adding la this week. Two pitches is the win.`,`Tiny hand signs no one can see. Make them clear.`],challenge:`Teacher points to two friends: they sing a so–mi conversation (“Hi.” “Hi.”).`,worksheet:{prompt:`Circle the higher one.`,items:[{id:`high`,kind:`choice`,label:`Which is higher?`,choices:[`so`,`mi`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`A so–mi song we sang today:`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:5,unit:`voice`,short:`Games`,title:`Singing Games`,focus:`A song can be a game. We sing, we move, we take turns, we keep the tune.`,outcome:`Students sing in tune many rhythmic and melodic songs, singing games and action songs.`,materials:[`Circle space`,`One singing game (Lucy Locket or Bounce High)`,`Beanbag or small ball optional`],warmup:{title:`Bounce High, Bounce Low`,detail:`Seated bounce on the beat, then standing. Words: “Bounce high, bounce low, bounce the ball to Shiloh.” Swap in a class name. So–mi–la sneaks in; do not lecture it yet.`},teach:[{title:`A singing game has a job`,detail:`We sing the whole time. The game is not a recess shout. If the tune falls apart, freeze, sing once sitting, then play again.`},{title:`Lucy Locket`,detail:`Circle. One child walks the beat on the outside with a “pocket” (beanbag). At the end of the verse they drop it behind a friend. That friend walks next. Keep the singing going.`},{title:`Kind turns`,detail:`Everyone gets a turn over the year, not always today. We do not grab. We do not say “you missed.”`},{title:`Still a singing voice`,detail:`Excitement makes shouts. Remind: light voice, strong beat in the feet.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`We're Going on a Bear Hunt`,author:`Michael Rosen (illus. Helen Oxenbury)`,why:`Classic chanted/sung adventure with actions—song played as a class game.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Were-Going-Bear-Michael-Rosen/dp/0689853491`,guide:{"when": "Brain break between Teach and Play, standing in a circle (about 7 min).", "before": ["“Listen for the part that comes back again and again. That’s our chorus.”", "“In a singing game we keep our voices light, even when it gets exciting.”"], "during": ["Chant the chorus (“We’re going on a bear hunt…”) with a pat-clap steady beat. Everyone joins.", "“We can’t go over it. We can’t go under it. Oh no! We’ve got to go through it!” Hands go over, then under, on the beat.", "Give a small group a turn for each sound word: swishy swashy grass (rub palms), splash splosh river (pat laps), squelch squerch mud (gentle stamps), stumble trip forest, hoooo wooooo snowstorm (voices slide high and low), tiptoe cave (whisper).", "Run home fast, then freeze under the covers. Talk about how the tempo sped up."], "after": "Play Lucy Locket or Bounce High with the Bear Hunt rule: light voices, strong beat in the feet. Sit and sing once with no game.", "check": "“What part came back again and again? What happens to our voices in a singing game?”", "adapt": "Hold up picture cards (grass, river, mud, forest, snow, cave) so everyone knows what comes next. Children who need to move can lead the actions.", "extend": "Add a new obstacle, invent its sound word, and pick a classroom instrument for it."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0gyI6ykDwds", "channel": "Walker Books", "label": "Watch Michael Rosen perform it"}},activity:{title:`Play the game`,detail:`Run Lucy Locket or Bounce High for the middle of the block. Sit after two or three turns to reset the tune, then play again.`},close:`Sit. Sing the song once with no game. That is how we remember it. Stamp if the tune survived the game.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stand in a circle`,detail:`Hands to yourself. Eyes can follow the walker.`},{title:`Sing the whole time`,detail:`The game does not pause the song.`},{title:`Walk on the beat if you are the walker`,detail:`Not a run. Match the song.`},{title:`Kind hands`,detail:`No grabbing the beanbag. No pushing.`},{title:`Sit and sing it once more`,detail:`No game. Just the tune.`}],teacher:[`Pick walkers who need a win, not only the confident kids.`,`If the class cannot hold the tune and the game, drop the game and just sing this week. Try the game again in week 6.`,`Beanbags on the floor behind a child, not at their head.`],checklist:[`I sang during the game.`,`I kept my hands kind.`,`I can still sing the song sitting down.`],mistakes:[`Letting the game become tag. Freeze and reset.`,`Too many rules before the first play. Show once, play.`],challenge:`A pair sings Bounce High as a duet while the class pats the beat.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our singing game.`,items:[{id:`game`,kind:`text`,label:`The game we played:`,lines:1},{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`During a singing game I should`,choices:[`only run`,`sing the whole time`,`talk to my neighbour`]}]}},{n:6,unit:`voice`,short:`Together`,title:`We Can Sing Together`,focus:`Unison: one tune, many voices. This is hand-in project 1 — our class song.`,outcome:`Students sing accurately in unison and respond to a conductor’s start and stop.`,materials:[`The class song (See-Saw, Rain Rain, or Hello)`,`Quiet hand ready`,`Optional: record a take for families`],warmup:{title:`Start and stop`,detail:`Breathe together. Hand in: sing “loo” on so. Hand out: rest. Four tries. This is conducting for six-year-olds.`},teach:[{title:`Unison means one tune`,detail:`Not faster, not louder than your neighbour. Match. If you cannot hear the person beside you, you are too loud.`},{title:`Polish the class song`,detail:`Words, then tune, then both. Fix one muddy phrase by echo. Do not restart the whole song ten times — fix the sticky bar.`},{title:`Standing tall`,detail:`Feet quiet, faces toward the conductor. Hands still unless the song has actions.`},{title:`One sharing take`,detail:`Sing it through as if someone is listening. Celebrate the stop as much as the start.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Pete the Cat: I Love My White Shoes`,author:`Eric Litwin (illus. James Dean)`,why:`Repetitive class song invites everyone to sing the same tune in unison.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Pete-Cat-Love-White-Shoes/dp/0061906220`,guide:{"when": "Warm-up extension or hook, before you polish the class song (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Pete sings the same song again and again. Can we sing it as one voice, like one big Pete?”", "Practise hand in (start) and hand out (stop) once."], "during": ["You sing Pete’s “I love my white shoes” line the first time. On each repeat, bring the class in with your conductor hand and stop them with a closed hand.", "Before each page turn, ask what colour the shoes will be (red strawberries, blue blueberries, brown mud, wet water). Then sing the new colour together.", "On “Did Pete cry? Goodness, no!” the class answers in a speaking voice. Then everyone goes back to the singing voice.", "Unison check: “If you can’t hear the person beside you, you’re too loud.”"], "after": "Move to Project 1. Use the same hand in and hand out on the class song. Do two practice runs and one sharing run.", "check": "“What does unison mean?” (One tune, all together, no one louder or faster.)", "adapt": "The words barely change, so English learners can join after one page. Let a child who needs a job hold the colour cards.", "extend": "Add a verse with a new colour and a new mess. Sing it in unison."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QNAeRy6jS7c", "channel": "HarperKids", "label": "Watch read & sing-along"}},activity:{title:`Project 1 — class song`,detail:`Two practice runs, one “sharing” run. Optional: record on a classroom device to send home or play later. No solos required.`},close:`Name one thing the class did well (starts, soft voices, still feet). Stamp project 1 when the sharing run holds together.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch for the start`,detail:`Breathe when the teacher breathes. Sing on the hand.`},{title:`Match your neighbour`,detail:`Same tune. Same speed. Not a race.`},{title:`Use a singing voice`,detail:`Light. Not a shout.`},{title:`Stop together`,detail:`When the hand cuts off, the sound ends.`},{title:`Stand tall for the sharing run`,detail:`This is our class song.`}],teacher:[`Rubric is tiny: singing voice, start/stop, words. Do not grade pitch perfectly in week 6.`,`A child who mouths the words still belongs in the video.`,`If you record, check district photo/video rules first.`],checklist:[`I watched the teacher for the start.`,`I used a singing voice.`,`I stopped when the hand said stop.`],mistakes:[`Restarting for one cough. Keep going.`,`Picking a brand-new song today. Use one they know.`],challenge:`Add one action that matches a word, and keep the tune.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our class song.`,items:[{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`The song we shared:`,lines:1},{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`Unison means`,choices:[`everyone sings the same tune`,`everyone sings a different tune`,`only the teacher sings`]}]}},{n:7,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Feel the beat`,title:`Feel the Beat`,focus:`The beat is the steady heart of the song — even when the words are long or short.`,outcome:`Students understand that music may move to a steady beat and show it on their body.`,materials:[`Heart drawn on the board`,`A drum`,`A known song`],warmup:{title:`Heartbeat`,detail:`Hand on chest. Feel your own heart if you can. Teacher plays a slow steady drum. Students pat that same speed on their legs. “The drum is the heart of the music.”`},teach:[{title:`Beat does not stop for big words`,detail:`Say “cat, cat, cat, cat” on the beat. Then “elephant, cat, elephant, cat.” The lap pats stay even. The words change. That even pat is the beat.`},{title:`Show it three ways`,detail:`Pat. Step in place. Quiet two-finger clap. Switch on your cue so they do not get stuck in one motion.`},{title:`Song on top`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain or Hello while they pat. If pats follow the words instead of the beat, go back to drum only.`},{title:`The beat can be slow or faster`,detail:`Two tempos only today. Still even. Not “as fast as you can.”`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Hand, Hand, Fingers, Thumb`,author:`Al Perkins (illus. Eric Gurney)`,why:`Strong “dum ditty dum” pulse invites tapping a steady beat while reading.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Hand-Fingers-Thumb-Al-Perkins/dp/0394810767`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach (about 5 min). It’s short, so read it twice.", "before": ["Hand on chest: “Feel your heartbeat. Does the drum in this book keep an even beat?”", "“Listen for the words that repeat.”"], "during": ["First read: everyone pats a steady beat on laps the whole time. Chant “dum ditty dum ditty dum dum dum” together.", "Let one child play the hand drum on the beat for the monkeys’ drumming pages.", "Point out that the words change (rings on fingers, apples and plums, banjos that strum, fiddles that zum), but the pats stay even. That even pat is the beat.", "Second read: a little faster, but still even. Not “as fast as you can.”"], "after": "Go into Drum is the boss using the book’s chant. Fade the drum and see if the pats stay steady.", "check": "“Did your pats follow the words or the beat? Which one stayed even?”", "adapt": "If laps are too hard, tap shoulders or take steps in place. English learners can join on the repeated “dum ditty” line right away.", "extend": "Split the class: half keep the beat on drums or laps, half chant only the words."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pPWd1bVls8Q", "channel": "Bookworm Bonny"}},activity:{title:`Drum is the boss`,detail:`Teacher drum leads. Students match. Fade the drum and see if their pats stay even. Bring the drum back if they rush.`},close:`Ask a student to play four even beats on the drum. Class pats with them. Stamp if most pats stay even without the words pulling them.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Pat the drum’s speed`,detail:`Even pats. Not the words yet.`},{title:`Keep patting on long words`,detail:`Elephant still gets even pats.`},{title:`Try feet in place`,detail:`Soft shoes. Same speed as your hands.`},{title:`Sing and pat`,detail:`Song in your voice, beat in your body.`},{title:`Notice slow and faster`,detail:`Both are still a beat if they stay even.`}],teacher:[`Do not introduce ta yet. This week is only the even heart.`,`Sitting pats are easier than walking. Save walking for week 8.`,`A child who pats twice as fast is rushing — model beside them.`],checklist:[`I can pat a steady beat.`,`My pats stay even on long words.`,`I sang and patted at the same time.`],mistakes:[`Letting them pound. Quiet body percussion.`,`Talking about notes and stems. Not yet.`],challenge:`Pat the beat with eyes closed for eight beats. Did you stay with the drum?`,worksheet:{prompt:`The beat is the even heart.`,items:[{id:`beat`,kind:`choice`,label:`The beat is`,choices:[`even, like a heart`,`only the long words`,`as fast as I can go`]},{id:`show`,kind:`text`,label:`I can show the beat with my`,lines:1}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:8,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Walk it`,title:`Walk the Beat`,focus:`Take the steady beat into the feet. Walk, freeze, walk again.`,outcome:`Students move to the beat through walking, as appropriate to their psychomotor development.`,materials:[`Clear floor`,`Drum or song`,`Tape spots optional`],warmup:{title:`Seat beat, then stand`,detail:`Eight pats sitting. Stand. Eight steps in place. Sit. Repeat. Check shoes and space.`},teach:[{title:`Walk the drum`,detail:`Teacher plays even beats. Students walk the room on those beats. Not a race. Soft knees. Own space.`},{title:`Freeze is part of music`,detail:`Drum stops, feet stop. Practice five times. Celebrate still statues.`},{title:`The song walks too`,detail:`Sing a known song while walking the beat. If they skip to the words, go back to drum only.`},{title:`Directions`,detail:`Walk forward. Walk in a circle. Walk back to your spot by the last phrase. Give the “home” cue early.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Giraffes Can't Dance`,author:`Giles Andreae (illus. Guy Parker-Rees)`,why:`Gerald finds music he can move to—ideal for walking/dancing the beat with control.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Giraffes-Cant-Dance-Giles-Andreae/dp/0545392551`,guide:{"when": "Brain break right before Walk and freeze (about 7 min). Clear the floor space first.", "before": ["“Gerald thinks he can’t dance. Listen for what finally helps him move.”", "“Our feet follow the drum, not the words.”"], "during": ["At the Jungle Dance, give each animal its own drum groove. The class moves in place on the beat: warthogs waltzing (sway), rhinos rock ’n’ rolling, lions’ tango, chimps’ cha-cha-cha, baboons’ Scottish reel. Freeze at each page turn.", "When the animals laugh at Gerald, stop the drum. Everyone freezes.", "When the cricket plays his violin and says that sometimes when you’re different you just need a different song, play a slow, soft beat. Everyone sways gently.", "For Gerald’s big dance, take bigger moves that stay on the beat."], "after": "Walk and freeze: use a slow “moon music” beat and a faster “jungle dance” beat. End by walking home to spots and sitting on the last beat.", "check": "“Did your feet follow the drum or the words? What did you do when the drum stopped?”", "adapt": "Chair dancers can move arms and heads only. Pairs can walk side by side.", "extend": "Children pick a “different song” (drum, shaker, or piano on this site) and the class walks to its beat."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MXeKv1-wj_o", "channel": "KidTimeStoryTime"}},activity:{title:`Walk and freeze`,detail:`Three songs or drum grooves. Freeze on silence. End by walking home to spots and sitting on the last beat.`},close:`Sitting: “Did your feet follow the drum or the words?” Stamp if freezes are clean.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Find a safe space`,detail:`Arms not touching friends.`},{title:`Walk on the drum`,detail:`One step for each beat. Soft feet.`},{title:`Freeze when it stops`,detail:`Statue. No extra steps.`},{title:`Walk while we sing`,detail:`Feet stay even.`},{title:`Walk home on time`,detail:`Be on your spot by the last beat.`}],teacher:[`Gallop and skip wait until bodies are ready — many Grade 1s still need a walk.`,`If the room is tiny, walk in place or in a circle only.`,`A child who cannot walk the beat can pat on a chair and still be in.`],checklist:[`I walked on the beat.`,`I froze when the drum stopped.`,`I kept my body in my own space.`],mistakes:[`Turning it into tag.`,`A tempo they cannot walk. Slow is kind.`],challenge:`Be a quiet leader: four students match your walk without a drum, only your feet.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Feet on the beat.`,items:[{id:`freeze`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the drum stops I`,choices:[`keep walking`,`freeze`,`run`]},{id:`feel`,kind:`text`,label:`Walking the beat felt`,lines:1}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:9,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Hands and freeze`,title:`Hands, Feet, and Freeze`,focus:`Switch the beat between body parts. Control is the skill.`,outcome:`Students accompany singing with body percussion on the beat and freeze on cue.`,materials:[`Known song`,`Cue cards: hands / feet / freeze`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Simon beat`,detail:`“Beat in your hands.” “Beat in your feet.” “Beat on your shoulders.” Change every eight beats. Freeze on “rest.”`},teach:[{title:`The beat can move around the body`,detail:`Same even heart, new place. This is body percussion without fancy names yet.`},{title:`Shoulders and heads are gentle`,detail:`No hitting. Two fingers. Model the volume you want.`},{title:`Sing on top`,detail:`See-Saw or Engine, Engine. Teacher points to hands or feet. Class keeps the song going while the beat location changes.`},{title:`Freeze is a rest for the body`,detail:`Connect to next unit’s silence, but do not name ta yet.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Noisy Nora`,author:`Rosemary Wells`,why:`Nora’s noisy hands/feet then sudden silence support body beat plus freeze/control.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Noisy-Nora-Rosemary-Wells/dp/0140567283`,guide:{"when": "After Teach, before Cue-card beat (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Nora makes noise to get noticed. Listen for her noises and for the moment everything goes quiet.”", "“Show me where the beat is: hands? feet?”"], "during": ["After each noisy line (she bangs the window, slams the door, drops the marbles, knocks over the lamp, flies the kite down the stairs), the class plays four beats of body percussion. Call out where: hands, feet, shoulders (two fingers).", "On “Quiet!” and “Hush!” everyone freezes. Teacher reads the family’s lines. Students only do the freeze.", "When Nora says she’s leaving and there isn’t a sound, hold a long, still freeze.", "At the end, when she comes back with a crash, make one big clap and freeze."], "after": "Play Cue-card beat with a “NORA!” card. When it’s held up, freeze.", "check": "“Where was the beat when I said feet? What did we do on ‘Hush’?”", "adapt": "Show pictures or icons for HANDS, FEET, and FREEZE. Let a child who needs to move hold the cards.", "extend": "Add small percussion for Nora’s noises (drum for the door, shaker for the marbles). Everything freezes on “Hush.”"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WIrO90kQxG4", "channel": "Love2Read with Miss Ellis"}},activity:{title:`Cue-card beat`,detail:`A student helper holds HANDS or FEET. Teacher still controls freeze. Rotate the helper.`},close:`Name a child who changed on the first try. Stamp if the song survived the switches.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch the cue`,detail:`Hands, feet, or freeze.`},{title:`Keep the beat even`,detail:`A new body part is not a faster beat.`},{title:`Gentle hands`,detail:`We do not hurt our bodies to make a beat.`},{title:`Keep singing`,detail:`The song does not stop when the beat moves.`},{title:`Helper job`,detail:`If you hold a card, show it clearly and still sing.`}],teacher:[`Helpers who struggle with beat can still hold the card — that is a real job.`,`If noise spikes, sit and do only hands.`],checklist:[`I can put the beat in my hands or feet.`,`I freeze on cue.`,`I keep singing.`],mistakes:[`Too many body parts. Hands, feet, freeze is enough.`],challenge:`Switch every four beats instead of eight. Still even?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Where was the beat?`,items:[{id:`where`,kind:`text`,label:`Today the beat lived in my`,lines:1},{id:`gentle`,kind:`choice`,label:`Body percussion should be`,choices:[`gentle`,`as hard as I can`,`only on a friend`]}]}},{n:10,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Strong beats`,title:`Strong Beats and Quiet Beats`,focus:`Some beats feel heavier. We can show a strong beat without getting loud and wild.`,outcome:`Students understand that there are strong and weak beats in music.`,materials:[`Drum`,`A 2-beat song (See-Saw) and a 4-beat chant`,`Board: STRONG quiet STRONG quiet`],warmup:{title:`March in place`,detail:`Left is stronger, right is lighter. “STRONG, quiet, STRONG, quiet.” Keep it small so knees stay safe.`},teach:[{title:`Strong is not shout`,detail:`A strong beat is a little heavier, not angry. Model a tiny knee bend on 1.`},{title:`Groups of 2`,detail:`See-Saw. Bend on “See,” light on “saw.” Pat-clap. Pat is strong.`},{title:`Groups of 4`,detail:`Engine, Engine. Strong on “En-gine” downbeats you choose — keep it simple: beat 1 is stronger, 2 3 4 stay light.`},{title:`Conducting`,detail:`Teacher’s hand is bigger on the strong beat. Students’ bodies match.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`The Loud Book!`,author:`Deborah Underwood (illus. Renata Liwska)`,why:`Catalogues many kinds of loud—pair with soft moments to contrast strong vs quiet beats.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Loud-Book-Deborah-Underwood/dp/0547390084`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Statue on 1 (about 6 min).", "before": ["“This book has lots of different louds. Is a strong beat a yelling beat?”", "Model STRONG–light–light–light with a tiny knee bend on beat 1."], "during": ["After each “loud” (alarm clock loud, applause loud, parade in the park loud, Aunt Tilly’s banjo band loud, fireworks loud), pat four beats. Make beat 1 heavier, not louder.", "For applause loud, clap beat 1 strong and beats 2–4 light. For parade in the park loud, march in place: STRONG, light.", "On “deafening silence loud,” freeze for four beats.", "If voices get wild, drop to a whisper. “Strong is not shout.”"], "after": "Statue on 1: walk the beat with a tiny freeze-frame on each strong beat. Then sit and pat-clap See-Saw (pat = strong).", "check": "“Is the strong beat the yelling beat?” (No. It’s a little heavier.)", "adapt": "Use a big hand and a small hand to show strong and light. Children can tap strong beats on a drum and light beats on their knee.", "extend": "Pair with The Quiet Book (week 16). Which kinds of quiet could still have a strong beat?"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3WRT9EQTqz4", "channel": "Kellogg Public Library"}},activity:{title:`Statue on 1`,detail:`Walk the beat. On each strong beat, a tiny freeze-frame, then keep going. Silly but controlled. Then sit and pat-clap a known song.`},close:`Ask: “Is the strong beat the yelling beat?” No. Stamp if strong/weak is visible in their bodies.`,studentSteps:[{title:`March in place`,detail:`One foot feels heavier.`},{title:`Pat-clap`,detail:`Pat = strong. Clap = quiet.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw with a bend on See`,detail:`Small bend.`},{title:`Watch the big hand`,detail:`That is beat one.`},{title:`Keep the quiet beats alive`,detail:`Do not skip them.`}],teacher:[`Do not say 2/4 and 4/4. Say groups of two and groups of four.`,`If they stomp, take shoes off the stomp and go back to finger pats.`],checklist:[`I can show a strong beat and a quiet beat.`,`I did not yell on the strong beat.`,`I kept the quiet beats going.`],mistakes:[`Only playing the strong beats. Weak beats matter.`],challenge:`Conduct the class with a big hand on 1 and small hands on the rest.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Strong and quiet.`,items:[{id:`strong`,kind:`choice`,label:`A strong beat should be`,choices:[`a little heavier`,`a shout`,`skipped`]},{id:`group`,kind:`choice`,label:`See-Saw feels like groups of`,choices:[`two`,`twenty`,`one long shout`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:11,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Beat vs words`,title:`Beat vs the Words`,focus:`The beat stays even. The rhythm is the pattern of the words. They are not the same.`,outcome:`Students begin to separate a steady beat from the rhythm of the words.`,materials:[`Board with two lines: beat (even dots) and words (long/short)`,`Known chants`],warmup:{title:`Two jobs`,detail:`Half the class pats even beats. Half speaks “Engine, engine, number nine.” Swap.`},teach:[{title:`Give them names`,detail:`Beat = even heart. Rhythm = the way the words go. We will clap the words next week as ta and ti-ti. Today we only feel the difference.`},{title:`Draw it`,detail:`Four even dots on the board. Under them, write Engine engine number nine and swoop the syllables. Dots stay even. Syllables bunch.`},{title:`Hands vs laps`,detail:`Laps keep the beat. Hands tap the words. This is hard. Do it slowly. Many will mix them — that is the lesson, not a failure.`},{title:`Song check`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain. Beat on laps. Then tap the words on shoulders. Ask which one stayed even.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Chicka Chicka Boom Boom`,author:`Bill Martin Jr. & John Archambault (illus. Lois Ehlert)`,why:`Chanted word-rhythms (“chicka chicka boom boom”) vs keeping a steady underlying beat.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Chicka-Boom-Bill-Martin-Jr/dp/1442450703`,guide:{"when": "Hook in Teach, just before Hands vs laps (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Two jobs today: laps keep the even beat, words make the rhythm.”", "Put four even dots on the board and have everyone touch the air as you point."], "during": ["First read: everyone chants “Chicka chicka boom boom! Will there be enough room?” while you pat the beat.", "Split the class. Half pat the beat. Half say “A told B, and B told C, I’ll meet you at the top of the coconut tree.” Swap on the next refrain.", "Write “chicka chicka boom boom” under the four dots. Notice how the syllables bunch up while the dots stay even.", "When the letters fall out of the tree, everyone freezes. Restart the beat together."], "after": "Split the class: Line A plays the beat, Line B chants the words. Use Engine, Engine too. End all together on the beat.", "check": "“Which one stayed even: the laps or the words?” (Laps, the beat.)", "adapt": "Let children who find two jobs hard do only the beat. English learners can chant just “boom boom.”", "extend": "Play the words on rhythm sticks and the beat on a drum at the same time."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=quohQa3lz40", "channel": "Buddy Son Storytime"}},activity:{title:`Split the class`,detail:`Line A: beat. Line B: words. Conduct them in and out. Swap. End all together on beat only, to land on something they can succeed at.`},close:`“Which one is even?” Beat. Stamp if they can point to it.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Pat four even beats`,detail:`Heart.`},{title:`Speak the chant`,detail:`Words have their own pattern.`},{title:`Watch the board`,detail:`Dots stay even. Words do not.`},{title:`Try two jobs with a partner`,detail:`One beat, one words. Then swap.`},{title:`Finish on the beat`,detail:`Everyone pats together.`}],teacher:[`This is the conceptual leap of Grade 1 rhythm. Do not rush to notation if they are wobbly.`,`Partners help. Choose them.`],checklist:[`I know the beat is even.`,`I know the words can be long and short.`,`I tried one job while a friend did the other.`],mistakes:[`Using the word “rhythm” as a scold (“keep the rhythm”). Today it has a precise meaning.`],challenge:`Tap the words to your name. Does your name fit in four beats?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Beat or words?`,items:[{id:`even`,kind:`choice`,label:`The even heart is the`,choices:[`beat`,`words`]},{id:`name`,kind:`text`,label:`Clap the way your name goes. Write your name:`,lines:1}]}},{n:12,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Parade`,title:`Beat Parade`,focus:`Hand-in project 2: walk or play a steady beat, freeze on silence, stay safe.`,outcome:`Students maintain a steady beat with body or instrument and freeze on rest.`,materials:[`Classroom percussion for half the class if you have it`,`Parade path`,`Known song`],warmup:{title:`Silent parade`,detail:`Walk the beat with no talking. Drum only. Freeze. Sit.`},teach:[{title:`What “done” looks like`,detail:`Even feet or even instrument. Freeze on stop. Instruments stay quiet until the start hand.`},{title:`If you have a shaker or sticks`,detail:`Play the beat, not the words. Two-finger volume. Sticks are not swords.`},{title:`The song is the street`,detail:`Teacher sings. Parade moves. At the fermata or drum stop, statues.`},{title:`Audience spots`,detail:`Half parade, half watch. Watchers check: even? safe? freeze? Swap.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Max Found Two Sticks`,author:`Brian Pinkney`,why:`Max’s steady drumming joins a marching band parade—perfect parade-with-beat lesson.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Found-Sticks-Brian-Pinkney/dp/068981593X`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach (about 7 min).", "before": ["“Max doesn’t talk. He answers with his sticks. Listen for the sounds he copies.”", "Practise instrument rest position once before reading."], "during": ["For each object Max plays, model a short pattern. The class echoes on laps: his thighs (pigeons), Grandpa’s bucket (rain on the window), the hatboxes (tom-toms in a marching band), the soda bottles (church bells), the garbage cans (the train).", "Keep a steady drum beat under the whole book so the parade feeling builds.", "When the marching band comes around the corner (“thump dee dee thump”), stand and march in place on the beat.", "When the drummer tosses Max the sticks, freeze. Then sit."], "after": "Project 2, Beat parade: two rehearsal parades and one sharing parade. Watchers say “I saw even feet.”", "check": "“Did Max play the beat when the band came by? How did you show it?”", "adapt": "Children who find marching hard can stay seated and play the beat on laps or a drum.", "extend": "Children choose one of Max’s objects and play its pattern on a classroom instrument inside the parade."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EoP8RXcZWQg", "channel": "The Cleveland Orchestra"}},activity:{title:`Project 2 — parade`,detail:`Two rehearsal parades, one sharing parade. Watchers give one kind comment: “I saw even feet.”`},close:`Collect instruments carefully — that is still music class. Stamp project 2.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Wait for the start`,detail:`Instrument down until the hand.`},{title:`Play or walk the beat`,detail:`Even. Not the words.`},{title:`Freeze on stop`,detail:`No extra shake.`},{title:`Safe tools`,detail:`Sticks down when you walk to your seat.`},{title:`Watchers watch for even feet`,detail:`Say one kind thing.`}],teacher:[`Not enough instruments? Body percussion is a full project.`,`Rubric: even beat, freeze, safety. That is all.`],checklist:[`My beat stayed even.`,`I froze on the stop.`,`I used my instrument or body with care.`],mistakes:[`A long winding parade in a crowded room. A circle is enough.`],challenge:`Play the beat on a drum while the class walks. You are the heart.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Beat parade.`,items:[{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`In the parade I`,choices:[`kept an even beat`,`played as fast as I could`,`chased a friend`]},{id:`proud`,kind:`text`,label:`I am proud that I`,lines:2}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:13,unit:`melody`,short:`Long short`,title:`Long and Short`,focus:`Sounds can be long or short. We hear it, we draw it, we clap it.`,outcome:`Students understand that music is made up of long sounds, short sounds and silences.`,materials:[`Board`,`Yarn or drawn lines (long dash, two short dashes)`,`Voices`],warmup:{title:`Stretch and tap`,detail:`Slide a long “oooo” with arms opening. Then two short “ti ti” finger taps. Alternate.`},teach:[{title:`Long takes more time`,detail:`Hold “luuuu.” Short is “ti-ti.” Both can sit on a beat: one long fills a beat, two shorts share a beat. Say it in kid words: one long sound for one step, two quick sounds for one step.`},{title:`Draw icons`,detail:`A long macaroni. Two little dots. No stems required. Grade 1 can write these.`},{title:`Names come next week`,detail:`Today: long and short. Tomorrow: ta and ti-ti. Let the ears go first.`},{title:`Song hunt`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain. Which words feel longer? “Rain” can be long. “Go a-way” has shorter bits. Do not over-analyse.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Charlie Parker Played Be Bop`,author:`Chris Raschka`,why:`Playful long and short sound syllables make duration audible for young listeners.`,linkType:`youtube`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GeAHASFqvb4`,guide:{"when": "Warm-up extension or quick hook (about 4 min). The book is very short, so read or play it twice.", "before": ["“Listen for sounds that stretch long and sounds that pop short.”", "Model a long “ooo” with arms opening and a short “ti” finger tap."], "during": ["Second time through: stretch arms for long sounds, like the end of “Never leave your cat a-lone” and the buzzing “zznnzznn.”", "Tap fingers for short sounds: “be bop,” “fisk, fisk,” “lollipop,” “boomba, boomba,” and the one-sound “BANG!”", "Echo the playful word chains (“overshoes, overshoes…,” “chickadee, chickadee…”). Keep a steady pat underneath.", "Ask: “Was that word long or short?” Answer with arms or fingers, not voices."], "after": "Compose with yarn: pairs lay long and short sounds into four beats, then say them with book words (long “a-lone,” short “bop bop”).", "check": "Teacher says “bop” or “a-looone.” Students show short (finger tap) or long (arms). Most correct = stamp.", "adapt": "Use the arm and finger gestures for everything so English learners don’t need the words.", "extend": "Play 30 seconds of a Charlie Parker recording. Do you hear long notes, short notes, or both?"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RMSFqWJ_PrA", "channel": "The Rabbit Hole Kansas City", "label": "Watch Chris Raschka perform it"}},activity:{title:`Compose with yarn`,detail:`Pairs get four beats of space on the floor or paper. They lay one long or two shorts in each beat. Clap it. Switch with a neighbour.`},close:`Class claps one pair’s pattern. Stamp if they can tell long from short by ear.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Make a long sound`,detail:`Stretch it. Do not shout it.`},{title:`Make two short sounds`,detail:`They share one step.`},{title:`Draw long and short`,detail:`Macaroni and two dots.`},{title:`Build four beats`,detail:`With a partner. Then clap it.`},{title:`Listen to another pair`,detail:`Copy their pattern.`}],teacher:[`Keep silences for week 16. Too many ideas at once will blur.`,`If you use real notation, still say long and short out loud.`],checklist:[`I can make a long sound and short sounds.`,`I can draw them.`,`I clapped a four-beat idea.`],mistakes:[`Calling everything “notes.” Use long and short.`],challenge:`Sing your four-beat picture on so–mi.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw four beats. Use long lines and short-short pairs.`,items:[{id:`draw`,kind:`text`,label:`My four beats (describe or list long/short):`,lines:3},{id:`ear`,kind:`choice`,label:`Two short sounds in one beat are`,choices:[`faster little sounds`,`two long sounds`,`a freeze`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:14,unit:`melody`,short:`Ta`,title:`Ta`,focus:`The long sound that fills one beat has a name: ta. We say it, clap it, and see it.`,outcome:`Students use ta (quarter note) to show one sound on one beat.`,materials:[`Icon or stemless ta on the board (stick or apple)`,`Known 4-beat chant`],warmup:{title:`Four tas`,detail:`Say “ta ta ta ta” while stepping. Clap it. Whisper it. That is the whole warmup.`},teach:[{title:`Ta is one sound on one beat`,detail:`Point to four tas on the board. Touch each as you say it. Students write four tas in the air.`},{title:`Icon they can draw`,detail:`A simple stick, a heart, or a stemless note. Pick one and stay with it all year.`},{title:`Find ta in a chant`,detail:`“One, two, tie my shoe” — many tas. Pat and say ta instead of words, then put words back.`},{title:`Read a line`,detail:`Board: ta ta ta ta. Class reads. Change beat 4 later in the block when rest arrives in week 16 — not yet.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`This Jazz Man`,author:`Karen Ehrhardt (illus. R.G. Roth)`,why:`Counting chant (“this jazz man, he plays one”) supports one sound per beat (ta) while singing.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/This-Jazz-Man-Karen-Ehrhardt/dp/0544339223`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Write four tas (about 7 min). It’s sung to “This Old Man.”", "before": ["“This book is a song you already know, ‘This Old Man.’ Listen for the numbers.”", "Put four tas on the board: ta ta ta ta."], "during": ["Sing each verse. Students step or pat ta on every beat.", "On “give the man a hand,” clap four even tas while you point to the board.", "Echo each musician’s sound words (snap-snap for the thumb, tap-shuffle for the shoes, bippity bop for the conga). The ta beat stays steady on laps underneath.", "At ten, the band plays “again,” so sing the ending one more time with everyone clapping tas."], "after": "Write four tas. Swap “give the man a hand” for “ta ta ta ta” and read a friend’s card.", "check": "“How many sounds go on one beat when it’s a ta?” (One.)", "adapt": "Children who can’t sing and step at the same time can just step the beat. The counting helps English learners.", "extend": "The back of the book names the real players (Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Charlie Parker, and more). Play 20 seconds of one of them."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bSr1HEvcMhU", "channel": "KidTimeStoryTime", "label": "Watch read/sung aloud"}},activity:{title:`Write four tas`,detail:`On the worksheet or mini whiteboards, four tas. Walk and read a friend’s. Play them on laps.`},close:`One child points, class says ta. Stamp if the pointing matches the speaking.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Say ta four times`,detail:`Even. With your feet if you want.`},{title:`Draw ta the class way`,detail:`Copy the board.`},{title:`Read the board`,detail:`Point and say.`},{title:`Put ta under a chant`,detail:`Words, then tas, then words.`},{title:`Read a friend’s tas`,detail:`Four beats.`}],teacher:[`Do not introduce the quarter-note name unless you love it. Ta is enough in Grade 1.`,`Keep reading left to right. Some children will poke at random.`],checklist:[`I can say ta.`,`I can draw ta.`,`I can read four tas.`],mistakes:[`Writing eight tiny tas in one beat. One ta, one beat.`],challenge:`Conduct four tas with a straight finger, left to right.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write four tas. Point as you say them.`,items:[{id:`tas`,kind:`text`,label:`My four tas:`,lines:2},{id:`mean`,kind:`choice`,label:`Ta means`,choices:[`one sound on one beat`,`two sounds on one beat`,`no sound`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:15,unit:`melody`,short:`Ti-ti`,title:`Ti-ti`,focus:`Two short sounds that share one beat: ti-ti.`,outcome:`Students use ti-ti (two eighth notes) as two sounds on one beat.`,materials:[`Board: ta vs ti-ti`,`Chants with pairs of shorts (Pease Porridge Hot)`],warmup:{title:`Slow ta, quick ti-ti`,detail:`Step ta. Jog in place ti-ti on the same beat speed — two little steps in the time of one. Hold a chair if jogging is too much; two finger taps instead.`},teach:[{title:`Ti-ti shares the beat`,detail:`One ta = one sandwich. Ti-ti = two halves. They take the same plate (the beat).`},{title:`Draw two little notes together`,detail:`Two sticks with a beam, or two dots with a line. Stay consistent with week 14’s icon style.`},{title:`Pease Porridge Hot`,detail:`Clap the words. Find the quick pairs. Say them as ti-ti. Keep the beat in the feet.`},{title:`Read mixed lines`,detail:`ta ta ti-ti ta. Point. If they say ti-ti too slowly, it becomes two tas — fix it kindly with a model.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Tanka Tanka Skunk!`,author:`Steve Webb`,why:`Two-syllable names (Tan-ka) vs one-syllable (Skunk) map cleanly to ti-ti and ta.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Tanka-Skunk-Steve-Webb/dp/0439578442`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Say the animal names. Do they have one sound or two sounds on a beat?”", "Review: one finger = ta, two fingers = ti-ti."], "during": ["Chant “Tan-ka Tan-ka Skunk” with the beat on laps. Tanka is ti-ti and Skunk is ta. Rest on the next beat: ti-ti ti-ti ta sh.", "For each animal page, say the name and show one or two fingers: skunk, fox, yak, ox = ta; tiger, cheetah, llama, zebra, panda = ti-ti.", "Longer names like kangaroo and caterpillar: just enjoy them. No need to notate.", "Play the Tanka chant on a drum and the Skunk beat on a shaker."], "after": "Build a bar: fill four beat boxes with animal names from the book (e.g., “tiger, skunk, zebra, fox”). Clap and read them as ta and ti-ti.", "check": "Teacher says “skunk” or “tiger.” Students hold up one finger (ta) or two (ti-ti).", "adapt": "Show animal pictures with dots underneath (one dot or two). Wigglers can step ta and jog ti-ti.", "extend": "Children invent their own four-beat animal parade and play it on drums."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Ng3QOLKXxs", "channel": "Mr. Simon Music"}},activity:{title:`Build a bar`,detail:`Four beat boxes on paper. Fill each with ta or ti-ti. Clap. Trade papers.`},close:`Ear quiz: teacher claps ta or ti-ti. Students hold one finger or two. Stamp if most are right.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Clap ti-ti`,detail:`Two quick claps in one beat.`},{title:`Draw ti-ti`,detail:`Two friends in one box.`},{title:`Read ta ta ti-ti ta`,detail:`Point as you go.`},{title:`Find ti-ti in a chant`,detail:`The quick words.`},{title:`Fill four boxes`,detail:`Ta or ti-ti in each.`}],teacher:[`The error will be ti-ti as two full beats. Use the sandwich plate picture again.`,`No rest yet unless they ask. “Empty box” is next week.`],checklist:[`I can clap ti-ti in one beat.`,`I can read a line with ta and ti-ti.`,`I can hear the difference.`],mistakes:[`Writing ti-ti as two tas in two boxes.`],challenge:`Make a pattern that uses both ta and ti-ti and teach it to your row.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Fill four beats with ta or ti-ti.`,items:[{id:`line`,kind:`text`,label:`My pattern (write ta and ti-ti):`,lines:2},{id:`share`,kind:`choice`,label:`Ti-ti means`,choices:[`two sounds in one beat`,`one long sound`,`stop`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:16,unit:`melody`,short:`Rest`,title:`Sh — the Rest`,focus:`Silence is music too. A rest is one beat of no sound, with a still body.`,outcome:`Students show silences as a one-beat rest (sh) inside a four-beat pattern.`,materials:[`Board: rest icon (z or a sh finger)`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Play, rest, play, rest`,detail:`Clap, open hands (silence), clap, open hands. The open hands are the rest. Faces stay ready — rest is not slump.`},teach:[{title:`Rest is a beat with no sound`,detail:`We still feel it. We do not skip it. We do not say “sh” loudly if that becomes a sound — a finger on lips or open palms works.`},{title:`Write it`,detail:`A squiggle, a Z, or the traditional rest. Pick one.`},{title:`Read ta ta rest ta`,detail:`The tricky part is staying still for the full beat. Count it in your head.`},{title:`Put a rest in a known chant`,detail:`Make beat 4 silent in Engine, Engine. Giggle once, then make the silence real.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`The Quiet Book`,author:`Deborah Underwood (illus. Renata Liwska)`,why:`Celebrates many kinds of quiet—ideal metaphor for musical silence and the rest.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Quiet-Book-Deborah-Underwood/dp/0547215673`,guide:{"when": "Close, or just before Mystery rest (about 5 min).", "before": ["“A rest is a beat with no sound. We still feel it.”", "Practise open hands on the rest: clap, clap, clap, open."], "during": ["Before each page, the class claps ta ta ta and opens hands on beat 4. Then read that kind of quiet.", "Pause on the pages where the quiet has a still body: hide-and-seek quiet, top of the roller coaster quiet, right before you yell surprise quiet, before the concert starts quiet.", "Remind them that a rest isn’t a slump. Bodies stay ready, like right before you yell surprise.", "Read the last pages (story time quiet, bedtime kiss quiet, sound asleep quiet) more and more softly."], "after": "Mystery rest: clap four beats with one silent beat. Students point to the silent beat (1, 2, 3, or 4), then write their own pattern with one rest.", "check": "“Which beat was the rest? Did we skip it, or feel it?”", "adapt": "Children can hold up a picture of a “shh” finger during rests. Keep one quiet page per clap pattern so it stays short.", "extend": "Children invent their own kind of quiet (“waiting for recess quiet”) and perform a four-beat pattern with one rest."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9u9GatEwxM", "channel": "Langworthy Public Library"}},activity:{title:`Mystery rest`,detail:`Teacher claps a four-beat pattern with one rest. Students point to which beat was silent (1, 2, 3, or 4). Then they write their own with one rest and perform.`},close:`Class statue for one full rest. Stamp if rests keep their full beat.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show a silent beat`,detail:`Open hands. No extra clap.`},{title:`Read ta ta sh ta`,detail:`The sh is a full beat.`},{title:`Write one rest in four beats`,detail:`Then clap it.`},{title:`Guess the silent beat`,detail:`Listen to the teacher.`},{title:`Keep your body ready`,detail:`Rest is not falling asleep.`}],teacher:[`If they giggle through rests, make a game: who can keep the quietest rest?`,`Do not combine this with new pitches today.`],checklist:[`I can show a rest with no sound.`,`My rest lasts a whole beat.`,`I can read a line with a rest.`],mistakes:[`Rushing past the rest. Put your hand on the icon for the full beat.`],challenge:`A pattern with two rests that still feels like music.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write four beats. Include one rest.`,items:[{id:`line`,kind:`text`,label:`My pattern (ta, ti-ti, sh):`,lines:2},{id:`rest`,kind:`choice`,label:`A rest is`,choices:[`a beat of no sound`,`a chance to talk`,`the end of class`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:17,unit:`melody`,short:`Patterns`,title:`Four-Beat Patterns`,focus:`Put ta, ti-ti, and rest together. Read, clap, play.`,outcome:`Students read and perform four-beat patterns using ta, ti-ti, and rest.`,materials:[`Flash patterns`,`Rhythm sticks or laps`,`Studio on this site`],warmup:{title:`Flash cards`,detail:`Three cards: all tas; ta ti-ti ta ta; ta ta sh ta. See, wait, clap. No talking between.`},teach:[{title:`Eyes, then hands`,detail:`We read first in our heads. Then we clap. This stops blurting.`},{title:`Play it on sticks`,detail:`Same pattern, new timbre. Quiet sticks.`},{title:`Error hunt`,detail:`Teacher claps a written pattern with one mistake. Students catch it. Kind voices: “Beat 3 was a rest on the paper.”`},{title:`Link to a song`,detail:`Use a pattern as an ostinato under Rain, Rain — teacher sings, a small group plays the pattern twice.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`The Jazz Fly`,author:`Matthew Gollub (illus. Karen Hanke)`,why:`Scat and drum patterns invite echoing and building short rhythmic (four-beat) ideas.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Jazz-Fly-Matthew-Gollub/dp/1889910171`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Write, trade, play (about 7 min). The author’s audio has music and sound.", "before": ["“The fly is lost and asks animals for directions. Listen for the sounds they answer with.”", "Put four beat boxes on the board."], "during": ["Turn each animal answer into a four-beat pattern and clap it: frog “rib-bit rib-bit” (ti-ti ti-ti), hog “oink, oink” (ta ta), donkey “hee-haw,” dog “ruff.”", "Write one pattern in the boxes, e.g. “rib-bit, oink, rib-bit, sh” = ti-ti ta ti-ti rest. Read it, clap it, then play it on sticks.", "When the Queen Bee demands a new beat, stop and ask: “How will the fly make one?”", "When the fly mixes the animal sounds into his drumming, the class performs two of their patterns back to back."], "after": "Write, trade, play: each child writes four beats using animal words (at least two kinds: ta, ti-ti, rest). Partner plays it.", "check": "“How many beats are in our pattern? Which beat is the rest?”", "adapt": "Provide animal picture cards with the rhythm printed underneath. English learners can say the animal sounds even without the words.", "extend": "Layer two patterns at once, half the class on each, like the fly’s band."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=84PNWvD3wJI", "channel": "Matthew Gollub (author)", "label": "Listen: author’s full audio"}},activity:{title:`Write, trade, play`,detail:`Each child writes four beats using at least two kinds (ta / ti-ti / sh). Trade with a partner. Play your partner’s.`},close:`Perform two student patterns as a class. Stamp if reading is becoming independent.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Read with your eyes first`,detail:`No sound yet.`},{title:`Clap the card`,detail:`Then sticks if you have them.`},{title:`Catch a mistake`,detail:`Listen. Look at the paper.`},{title:`Write your own four beats`,detail:`Use two kinds at least.`},{title:`Play a friend’s pattern`,detail:`That is reading.`}],teacher:[`Walk the room and rewrite backwards letters or eight tas in one box.`,`Ostinato groups should be three or four children, not the whole loud class.`],checklist:[`I read before I clapped.`,`I wrote a four-beat pattern.`,`I played a friend’s pattern.`],mistakes:[`Patterns longer than four. Four is the job.`],challenge:`Play your pattern twice without a hole in the middle. That is an ostinato.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write two different four-beat patterns.`,items:[{id:`p1`,kind:`text`,label:`Pattern A:`,lines:1},{id:`p2`,kind:`text`,label:`Pattern B:`,lines:1},{id:`kinds`,kind:`choice`,label:`I used`,choices:[`only ta`,`ta and ti-ti or rest`,`scribbles`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:18,unit:`melody`,short:`Compose`,title:`My Four-Beat Pattern`,focus:`Hand-in project 3: one four-beat pattern the class can echo.`,outcome:`Students create a simple rhythmic composition using known elements.`,materials:[`Worksheet`,`Laps or sticks`,`A way to notate (icons)`],warmup:{title:`Echo warmup`,detail:`Teacher claps four, class echoes. Student volunteer claps four, class echoes. Set the project mechanic.`},teach:[{title:`The recipe`,detail:`Four beats. At least two of: ta, ti-ti, rest. Must be echoable — not so wild the class cannot copy.`},{title:`Practice alone`,detail:`Write. Clap. Fix. If you cannot clap it, change it.`},{title:`How we share`,detail:`Child claps. Class echoes once. Kind applause (two claps). Next child. You will not get through 24 solos in 12 minutes — do a row today, another row if you have extra time, or split with week 19’s warmup.`},{title:`If they freeze`,detail:`They may clap all tas. That still counts. Pride matters more than complexity.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Jam, Too?`,author:`JaNay Brown-Wood (illus. Jacqueline Alcántara)`,why:`A child invents and joins percussion patterns—supports composing your own 4-beat idea.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Jam-Too-JaNay-Brown-Wood/dp/059332376X`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach, before the recipe (about 6 min).", "before": ["“A man plays a conga on the shore. Listen for how each new person asks to join.”", "“Everyone brings their own sound. Today you bring your own four-beat pattern.”"], "during": ["Each time someone arrives, the class chants “Can I jam too?” and you answer “Sure thing, my friend!”", "Echo the drum sounds (slap slap, tip tap) as four-beat body patterns.", "Add one classroom instrument for each new player: drum for the conga, then a djembe, a shaker, finger cymbals or a triangle for the zills, maracas, and bongos. Layer them on a steady beat.", "The child in the story has no drum and chooses to dance instead. Invite a few children to dance on the beat."], "after": "Project 3 share. Before each child claps, the class asks “Can you jam too?” The child claps their pattern and the class echoes it once.", "check": "“What does your pattern need?” (Four beats, at least two of ta, ti-ti, rest, and the class must be able to echo it.)", "adapt": "Dancing, or clapping all tas, still counts as joining the jam. English learners can join the repeated question right away.", "extend": "Put three student patterns in a row to make a class “jam” and play it on instruments."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aPhoQ-y1D7c", "channel": "DeSotoTrailSchool"}},activity:{title:`Project 3 — share`,detail:`As many students as time allows. Teacher notes who still needs a turn next class. Collect papers.`},close:`Name the musical bravery, not the fanciest pattern. Stamp project 3 as you go, or stamp the week when papers are in.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Write four beats`,detail:`Ta, ti-ti, sh. At least two kinds if you can.`},{title:`Clap it yourself`,detail:`If it is too hard, change it.`},{title:`Clap for the class`,detail:`Clear and once.`},{title:`Echo your friends`,detail:`Copy them carefully.`},{title:`Hand in the paper`,detail:`Name on it.`}],teacher:[`Do not force a solo from a child who is not ready. They can clap with you in unison as their share.`,`Rubric: four beats, readable, echoable. That is a 4-point load if you split as in the project sheet.`],checklist:[`My pattern is four beats.`,`I clapped it.`,`I echoed someone else.`],mistakes:[`Using this day to teach a new note. No new content.`],challenge:`Add your pattern under a known song as a quiet ostinato with two friends.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Project 3 — my four-beat pattern.`,items:[{id:`pattern`,kind:`text`,label:`Write ta / ti-ti / sh:`,lines:2},{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`The class could echo it`,choices:[`yes`,`I still need to make it simpler`,`not yet`]}]},studio:`rhythm`}],r=[{n:19,unit:`timbre`,short:`High low`,title:`High and Low`,focus:`Pitch can be high or low. We hear it, we show it with our bodies, and we sing it.`,outcome:`Students show that sounds may be high or low and match simple pitch changes with voice and movement.`,materials:[`Piano on this site or a real xylophone`,`Space to crouch and stretch`,`Board: high / low`],warmup:{title:`Mountain and valley`,detail:`Teacher slides a siren up. Students stretch tall. Siren down. Students crouch. No words. Do it four times.`},teach:[{title:`High is not loud`,detail:`A mouse can be high and quiet. A drum can be low and soft. Separate pitch from volume right away.`},{title:`Two notes only`,detail:`Play a high G and a low C (or so and do). Students point up or down. Then they sing “high” and “low” on those pitches.`},{title:`Xylophone or site piano`,detail:`If you have a glockenspiel, let two children play high and low while the class shows with bodies. If not, use the studio on this website.`},{title:`Song: Star Light`,detail:`Star Light, Star Bright sits in a small range. Listen for the drop. Trace it in the air.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Up, Up, Down`,author:`Robert Munsch (illus. Michael Martchenko)`,why:`Literal climbing up/down gives a clear high–low pitch metaphor for Grade 1.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Up-Down-Robert-Munsch/dp/1443113468`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Sort the sound (about 5 min).", "before": ["“Anna loves to climb. When she climbs up, let your voice slide up. When she falls, slide down.”", "“High is not loud. Keep your siren soft.”"], "during": ["Every time Anna goes “up, up, up,” slide your voice higher on each “up” while bodies stretch taller. On “down,” slide low and crouch.", "Point out that the words in the book climb up the page, just like her voice should.", "Keep the sirens quiet: a high sound can be soft, like a mouse.", "When Mom and then Dad try to climb the tree and fall, repeat the up–down slide. Two children can play high and low bars on a glockenspiel."], "after": "Sort the sound: stand for high, sit for low. Then pairs quiz each other with the site piano or a bell.", "check": "“If I sing softly, can it still be high?” (Yes.)", "adapt": "Use hands only (up and down) for children who can’t crouch. Point to the high and low parts of the picture of the tree.", "extend": "Children take turns being Anna: they play a high or low note and the class climbs or falls."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9rhuUXaqcIQ", "channel": "Robert Munsch (official audio)", "label": "Listen: Robert Munsch reads it"}},activity:{title:`Sort the sound`,detail:`Teacher plays or sings high/low in mixed order. Students stand for high, sit for low. Then pairs take the site piano or a bell and quiz their partner.`},close:`Ask: “If I sing softly, can it still be high?” Yes. Stamp if bodies match pitch, not volume.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stretch for high, crouch for low`,detail:`Follow the siren.`},{title:`Point up or down`,detail:`Listen to two notes.`},{title:`Sing high and low`,detail:`Soft is okay.`},{title:`Trace Star Light`,detail:`Finger draws the tune in the air.`},{title:`Quiz a partner`,detail:`Play one note. They show high or low.`}],teacher:[`Avoid “higher is better.” Low voices are music too.`,`A child who only mouths can still show with their body and be fully in.`],checklist:[`I can show high and low with my body.`,`I know high is not the same as loud.`,`I traced a melody in the air.`],mistakes:[`Using a tiny pitch difference they cannot hear. Make high and low obvious today.`],challenge:`Sing a so–mi “cuckoo” (high–low) and let the class echo.`,worksheet:{prompt:`High or low?`,items:[{id:`notloud`,kind:`choice`,label:`High means`,choices:[`up in pitch`,`loud`,`fast`]},{id:`show`,kind:`text`,label:`I show high by`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:20,unit:`timbre`,short:`Up and down`,title:`Melody Goes Up and Down`,focus:`A melody is a line. It can go up, down, or stay. We draw the line as we sing.`,outcome:`Students show melodic contour with movement and simple maps.`,materials:[`Paper for contour maps`,`A known so–mi song`,`Yarn optional`],warmup:{title:`Finger paint the air`,detail:`Teacher sings a slow so–mi–so. Students draw it in the air with one finger. Then a line that only goes up. Then only down.`},teach:[{title:`Contour means the shape`,detail:`We do not need note names to see the hill. Draw three maps on the board: hill, slide down, flat road.`},{title:`Map a known song`,detail:`See-Saw is a little up-down. Draw it together. Rain, Rain too.`},{title:`Same or different shape`,detail:`Sing two patterns. Thumbs up if the maps would look the same.`},{title:`Stay means repeats`,detail:`Ta ta on the same pitch is a flat line. That is still a melody.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Little Melba and Her Big Trombone`,author:`Katheryn Russell-Brown (illus. Frank Morrison)`,why:`Melba’s trombone melodies rise, fall, and soar—melodic contour in a musician’s story.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Little-Melba-Her-Big-Trombone/dp/1600608981`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 7 min). The book is long, so read up to Melba’s radio solo and save the rest for another day.", "before": ["“Listen for the sound words. Does each one go up, down, or stay the same?”", "Finger ready to draw in the air."], "during": ["On the sound-word page (the plink of a guitar, the hum of a bass, the thrum of a drum, the ping-pang of a piano, the tremble of a horn), say each word with a voice shape and trace it in the air.", "When Melba gets her trombone, slide your arm like the trombone slide while your voice slides up and down. That smooth line is contour.", "Her first try sounds like a howling dog: a wobbly line. After practice, a simple tune: a smooth hill. Draw both on the board.", "Ask: “Did that go up, down, or stay?”"], "after": "Draw the tune: students listen three times to a short pattern and draw one contour line. Check with a neighbour.", "check": "Hold up hill, slide, and flat-road cards. “Which map matches what I sang?”", "adapt": "English learners can use arm moves only. Children who need movement can walk a contour line on the floor.", "extend": "Play 20 seconds of a Melba Liston recording. Trace the trombone’s line in the air."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w7YqR6aZCUE", "channel": "Miami Children’s Museum"}},activity:{title:`Draw the tune`,detail:`Students listen three times to a short pattern and draw one contour line. Share with a neighbour: did your hills match?`},close:`Hold up two student maps. Class sings them as best they can. Stamp if drawings show direction.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Draw the air tune`,detail:`One finger. Slow.`},{title:`Name hill, slide, or road`,detail:`Look at the board.`},{title:`Map See-Saw`,detail:`Copy the class map, then try your own.`},{title:`Listen for same or different`,detail:`Thumbs up if the shape matches.`},{title:`Draw one mystery tune`,detail:`Listen three times.`}],teacher:[`Accept wobbly lines. Direction is the outcome, not art.`,`Do not require staff paper.`],checklist:[`I can draw a melody going up or down.`,`I mapped a song we know.`,`I compared my map with a friend.`],mistakes:[`Asking them to write note names on the staff today.`],challenge:`Sing a friend’s map without being told the words.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw the shape of a tune you heard.`,items:[{id:`map`,kind:`text`,label:`My melody map (describe: hill / slide / road):`,lines:2},{id:`dir`,kind:`choice`,label:`See-Saw mostly`,choices:[`goes up and down`,`stays on one shout`,`has no shape`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:21,unit:`timbre`,short:`So–mi songs`,title:`So–Mi Songs`,focus:`Put high and low into real songs. So–mi is the Grade 1 singing home.`,outcome:`Students sing so–mi songs in tune with hand signs.`,materials:[`Rain Rain, See-Saw, Star Light`,`Hand-sign space`],warmup:{title:`Sign and sing`,detail:`Silent signs, class sings. Then sung pattern, class signs. Keep it in so–mi only.`},teach:[{title:`Three songs, one pair of pitches`,detail:`Rain Rain, See-Saw, Star Light. They are family. Hum one and ask which song it might be.`},{title:`Fix the sticky phrase`,detail:`Do not run whole songs if phrase two is out of tune. Echo that phrase three times, then go back.`},{title:`Alone, then group`,detail:`A volunteer may sing a so–mi hello. The group answers. Sitting solos are allowed.`},{title:`Add a beat`,detail:`Laps on the beat while we sing. Two jobs again — easier now than week 11.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Rain, Rain, Go Away! (Sing and Read Storybook)`,author:`illustrated by Viviana Garofoli`,why:`Revisit the classic so–mi nursery song as class repertoire after introducing the pitches.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Rain-Away-Sing-Read/dp/0545463637`,guide:{"when": "Close, or as the song medley in Play (about 6 min). Sing the book.", "before": ["“Rain, Rain is part of our so–mi family. Which other songs sound like it?” (See-Saw, Star Light.)", "Silent signs first: so, mi, so, mi."], "during": ["Sing each page. The class shows so–mi hand signs on every “rain, rain.”", "Add a second job: pat the beat on one verse while singing. It should feel easier than in week 11.", "If one verse drifts out of tune, stop and echo just that phrase three times, then turn the page.", "Let a different child be the “hand teacher” for each verse (flowers in May, Saturday, the animals, the sun)."], "after": "Medley: sing Rain, Rain, See-Saw, and Star Light with signs. Pick one to perform standing in concert posture.", "check": "“Do your hand signs match what you’re singing?” Ask two children to show so and mi alone.", "adapt": "Children who aren’t ready to sign can point up and down. A shy singer can sign while the class sings.", "extend": "Hum the tune without words. Can the class name the song from the signs alone?"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LjeW3xOHiPg", "channel": "The Bookworms"}},activity:{title:`Song medley`,detail:`Sing all three songs with signs. Pick one to perform standing as if it were concert posture.`},close:`Which song felt easiest? That is their home song for later weeks. Stamp if signs and sound agree.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Warm up so and mi`,detail:`Hands and voice.`},{title:`Sing Rain, Rain`,detail:`Light voice.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw`,detail:`Small knee bend.`},{title:`Sing Star Light`,detail:`Quiet ending.`},{title:`Stand one of them`,detail:`Concert feet. Still hands unless there are actions.`}],teacher:[`If a class is shouty, sit and use a whisper-sing lead. They will drop.`,`Take a note of which song is strongest — that is a concert candidate for unit 6.`],checklist:[`I sang at least two so–mi songs.`,`My hand signs matched so and mi.`,`I used a singing voice, not a shout.`],mistakes:[`Adding la in the middle of a shaky so–mi. Wait one more week if they are not ready.`],challenge:`Lead See-Saw with only your hands. Class sings.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our so–mi songs.`,items:[{id:`songs`,kind:`text`,label:`Songs we sang today:`,lines:2},{id:`home`,kind:`choice`,label:`So is`,choices:[`higher`,`lower`,`louder`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:22,unit:`timbre`,short:`Add la`,title:`Add La`,focus:`La sits above so. Three pitches: so, mi, and la. Bounce High uses them.`,outcome:`Students respond to hand signals for so–mi–la and sing a three-pitch song.`,materials:[`Bounce High, Bounce Low`,`La hand sign (thumb up at top of head / curled so-hand higher — use one clear so-la-mi set and stick to it)`],warmup:{title:`So–mi neighbour`,detail:`Review so–mi. Then teacher sings so–la–so–mi. Students echo on “loo.”`},teach:[{title:`La is a step above so`,detail:`Show the sign higher than so. Students copy. Sing la lightly — it should not punch.`},{title:`Bounce High`,detail:`“Bounce high, bounce low, bounce the ball to Shiloh.” Teach by echo. Put a class name in. Keep the ball bounce on the beat if you use one.`},{title:`Sign the new note only`,detail:`First pass, only sign la when it happens. Then sign the whole song.`},{title:`Do not pile on do yet`,detail:`Three pitches is plenty. Hot Cross Buns can wait until they own la.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Up, Up, Down`,author:`Robert Munsch (illus. Michael Martchenko)`,why:`Climb phrases already used for so–mi can be extended by adding la on a third pitch.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Up-Down-Robert-Munsch/dp/1443113468`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Name toss (about 5 min). Munsch’s reading is short.", "before": ["“Anna climbs higher and higher. Today we have a new higher step: la, above so.”", "Sign mi, so, la from low to high."], "during": ["On “up, up, up,” sing mi–so–la on the three “ups” and show the signs. On “down,” come back to mi.", "When Anna reaches the top and shouts “I’m the king of the castle,” sing it on so–la–so–mi as a playground chant.", "Keep la light. It should float, not punch.", "When Mom and Dad fall, sing la–so–mi down the ladder. When Anna says “Be careful—don’t climb!” speak it."], "after": "Name toss with Bounce High: listen for la in “bounce the ball.”", "check": "Silent signs: so–la–so–mi. “Which one is the new note?” (La, the highest.)", "adapt": "Put a three-step ladder on the board (mi, so, la) and point as you sing. Hands-only signing is fine.", "extend": "Children sing “I’m the king of the castle” with their own name, using so–la–so–mi."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9rhuUXaqcIQ", "channel": "Robert Munsch (official audio)", "label": "Listen: Robert Munsch reads it"}},activity:{title:`Name toss`,detail:`Bounce High with a soft ball or beanbag. The named child sings the last “Shiloh” as their own name on the same tune if they want. Optional.`},close:`Silent signs: so–la–so–mi. Class sings. Stamp if la is in the neighbourhood of in-tune.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show la`,detail:`Higher than so. Light voice.`},{title:`Echo so–la–so–mi`,detail:`On loo.`},{title:`Sing Bounce High`,detail:`Put in a friend’s name if the teacher does.`},{title:`Sign la when it happens`,detail:`Then try all the signs.`},{title:`Keep the beat in your bounce`,detail:`The ball is the beat, not a throw at someone.`}],teacher:[`Kodály la is a curved hand above so. If you use a simpler “higher so,” be consistent all year.`,`Beanbag underhand. No dodgeball.`],checklist:[`I can show la.`,`I sang Bounce High.`,`I know la is higher than so.`],mistakes:[`A high la that becomes a squeal. Model a gentle one.`],challenge:`Improvise four beats on so–mi–la with your voice. Class copies.`,worksheet:{prompt:`So, mi, and la.`,items:[{id:`order`,kind:`choice`,label:`The highest of so, mi, and la is`,choices:[`la`,`mi`,`so`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`A song with la:`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:23,unit:`timbre`,short:`Hand signs`,title:`Hand Signs We Can Read`,focus:`The teacher’s hands are a score. We read so–mi–la without words.`,outcome:`Students read simple so–mi–la patterns from hand signs and sing them back.`,materials:[`Clear sightlines`,`No other props`],warmup:{title:`Copy my hands, no voice`,detail:`Silent sign strings of four. Students copy. Then add voice.`},teach:[{title:`Hands are the music`,detail:`You are not guessing. You are reading. Eyes on hands, then sound.`},{title:`Four-beat reading`,detail:`so so mi so. so la so mi. Rest can be closed fists. Keep it slow.`},{title:`Student conductor`,detail:`A child signs a pattern they know. Class sings. Help them stand where everyone can see.`},{title:`From signs to a song`,detail:`Sign the first phrase of Rain, Rain. They should recognize it. Magic moment — name it.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Do Re Mi: If You Can Read Music, Thank Guido D'Arezzo`,author:`Susan L. Roth`,why:`Picture-book story of solfa/notation that pairs with reading Curwen hand signs.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Do-Re-Mi-Susan-Roth/dp/0618465723`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 7 min). Summarise the middle pages. Read the start and the end.", "before": ["“Long ago, no one could write music down. How do you think children learned songs?” (By copying, like our echoes.)", "“Today our hands will be the music.”"], "during": ["When the choirmaster sings and the children repeat, stop and do a quick so–mi echo together.", "When Guido holds up his hand and says to pretend his fingers are lines, everyone holds up a hand. Notes go up in order from low to high.", "Sing mi, so, la moving your hand upward. Point out that our hand signs are another way to read music without paper.", "When the choir finally sings a song they’ve never heard just by reading it, cheer. That is what you’ll do today with signs."], "after": "Sign theatre: student conductors sign so–mi–la patterns and the class sings them. Sneak in the first phrase of Rain, Rain for them to discover.", "check": "Eyes closed: teacher sings so–mi–la–so. Students sign what they heard.", "adapt": "Pair a quieter child with a partner to sign together. English learners can follow the hand shapes without the words.", "extend": "Show a picture of a five-line staff and count the lines and spaces on a hand."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y_N9g9w-CyU", "channel": "Mrs. Scharpenberg"}},activity:{title:`Sign theatre`,detail:`Three student conductors. Class sings each pattern twice. Teacher sneaks in a known song phrase to be discovered.`},close:`All eyes closed. Teacher sings so–mi–la–so. Students sign what they heard. Stamp if signs match.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch first`,detail:`Hands, then voice.`},{title:`Sing four-beat patterns`,detail:`so, mi, la only.`},{title:`Conduct if you want`,detail:`Big clear signs.`},{title:`Guess the song from signs`,detail:`Rain, Rain or See-Saw maybe.`},{title:`Sign what you hear`,detail:`Eyes can close for the last one.`}],teacher:[`Stand on a chair only if it is safe and allowed. Otherwise a stool or just the front.`,`Fist rest is optional. Empty air also works.`],checklist:[`I watched the hands before I sang.`,`I sang a so–mi–la pattern from signs.`,`I recognized a song from its signs.`],mistakes:[`Patterns longer than four. Reading falls apart.`],challenge:`Sign a whole phrase of Bounce High. Can the class name it?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Reading hands.`,items:[{id:`read`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the teacher signs, I`,choices:[`watch, then sing`,`look at the floor`,`invent any tune`]},{id:`pattern`,kind:`text`,label:`A pattern we sang (write so mi la):`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:24,unit:`timbre`,short:`Little tune`,title:`A Little Melody`,focus:`Make a tiny so–mi–la tune, four beats long, and sing it for a friend.`,outcome:`Students create a short melody using known pitches and perform it.`,materials:[`Worksheet`,`Site piano`,`Optional bells`],warmup:{title:`Pitch bank`,detail:`Class sings so, mi, la as you point. Then they tap those three on the studio piano.`},teach:[{title:`Recipe`,detail:`Four beats. Use so, mi, and maybe la. Start and end on so or mi so it feels finished.`},{title:`Voice first`,detail:`If they write letters they cannot sing, it is not done. Hum it. Then write so/mi/la under four beats.`},{title:`Add rhythm they know`,detail:`Each beat is ta (one pitch) or ti-ti (two pitches). Rest is allowed.`},{title:`Share with a partner, not always the whole class`,detail:`Grade 1 composition is quiet and small. A few volunteers can share out.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Mole Music`,author:`David McPhail`,why:`Mole practices a small violin melody that grows—tiny tune becomes meaningful music.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Mole-Music-David-McPhail/dp/0805067663`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 6 min).", "before": ["“Mole’s first try is not beautiful. Listen for how he gets better.”", "“Today you’ll make a tiny tune, like Mole’s first song.”"], "during": ["When Mole first draws the bow and it screeches, the class makes one tiny squeak sound (then rests).", "“He could play one note, then two, then a scale”: sing so, then so–mi, then mi–so–la with signs.", "When he learns a simple song, you sing a four-beat so–mi–la tune. Class echoes. This is “Mole’s song.”", "Mole hums his music while he digs. Everyone hums the tune softly."], "after": "Write and hum: each child makes a four-beat tune with so, mi, and maybe la, starting and ending on so or mi. Teach it to a partner.", "check": "“Where does your tune start and end? Can you hum it before you write it?”", "adapt": "Offer a fill-in frame (so __ mi __) so beginners only pick two notes. Humming counts.", "extend": "Collect three “Mole songs” and play them on the site piano for the class."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G4GGVezr7RI", "channel": "Story Time with Charles"}},activity:{title:`Write and hum`,detail:`Compose. Try on piano. Teach your partner. Two volunteers share.`},close:`Collect tunes. We may reuse one in the concert unit. Stamp when papers have four beats of pitches.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Choose four beats of so, mi, la`,detail:`Start on so or mi if you can.`},{title:`Hum it`,detail:`If you cannot hum it, change it.`},{title:`Try it on the piano`,detail:`G is so, E is mi, A is la on our studio.`},{title:`Teach a partner`,detail:`They echo you.`},{title:`Write it down`,detail:`Name on the paper.`}],teacher:[`Do not require staff notation. Letter names or so-mi-la words are enough.`,`Save two strong little tunes for week 33’s class piece.`],checklist:[`My tune is four beats.`,`I can hum it.`,`A friend echoed it.`],mistakes:[`Eight different pitches. Three is the bank.`],challenge:`Put your tune over a ta ta ta ta beat that a friend pats.`,worksheet:{prompt:`My little melody.`,items:[{id:`tune`,kind:`text`,label:`Four beats (write so / mi / la):`,lines:2},{id:`start`,kind:`choice`,label:`I started on`,choices:[`so or mi`,`a shout`,`I am not sure`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:25,unit:`form`,short:`Body perc`,title:`Body Percussion`,focus:`Clap, pat, stamp, snap — four sounds we already own. Transfer the beat and simple patterns onto the body.`,outcome:`Students accompany singing with body percussion (beat and simple patterns) with care.`,materials:[`Open space`,`A known song`,`Quiet floor for stamps`],warmup:{title:`Copy four`,detail:`Clap clap pat pat. Class copies. Snap if they can; many Grade 1s cannot snap — two-finger tap on the palm is a snap substitute.`},teach:[{title:`Name the four`,detail:`Clap, pat (laps), stamp (gentle), snap or tap. Volume rule: we should still hear the song.`},{title:`Beat on the body`,detail:`Sing Hello or Rain, Rain. Beat in pats. Then beat in quiet stamps.`},{title:`Pattern on the body`,detail:`ti-ti ta rest on clap-clap pat open. This links unit 3 to instruments next week.`},{title:`Safety`,detail:`Stamps are not jumps. Snaps are not at someone’s ear.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Clap Your Hands`,author:`Lorinda Bryan Cauley`,why:`Clap, stomp, and move cues map directly to clap–pat–stamp–snap body percussion.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Clap-Hands-Lorinda-Bryan-Cauley/dp/0399221182`,guide:{"when": "Warm-up extension or brain break (about 5 min). Clear a little space.", "before": ["“Our four body sounds: clap, pat, stamp, snap (or finger tap). Listen for which one the book asks for.”", "“We should still hear the words over our sounds.”"], "during": ["Read the rhyming commands on a steady beat. Do each one four times: clap your hands, stomp your feet (gently), slap your knee (pat), wiggle your fingers (snap or tap).", "After each rhyming pair, play a four-beat body ostinato: clap–pat–clap–rest.", "On “quiet as a mouse, now don’t say a word,” freeze for a silent beat.", "Skip or change the lines that don’t fit a music room (blow a kiss instead of a kiss, no somersault: spin once slowly)."], "after": "Body ostinato: a small group repeats clap–pat–clap–rest while the class sings Rain, Rain. Swap groups.", "check": "“Name our four body sounds. Which one was the rest?”", "adapt": "Offer seated versions of every move. English learners can copy your actions without the words.", "extend": "Children write a four-beat body pattern with icons (hand, lap, foot) and the class performs it."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ayIDoHjlz0", "channel": "Brightly Storytime"}},activity:{title:`Body ostinato`,detail:`A small group repeats clap-pat-clap-rest while the class sings. Swap groups.`},close:`Freeze in a body-percussion statue. Stamp if volume stayed kind.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Copy clap, pat, stamp, tap`,detail:`Gentle.`},{title:`Put the beat in your laps`,detail:`Sing at the same time.`},{title:`Try a pattern`,detail:`ti-ti ta sh.`},{title:`Ostinato group`,detail:`Repeat without speeding up.`},{title:`Hear the singers`,detail:`If you cannot hear them, you are too loud.`}],teacher:[`Substitute snap from day one so no one feels broken for not snapping.`,`Carpet classes: stamp becomes a toe tap.`],checklist:[`I can clap, pat, and tap the beat.`,`I used a quiet stamp or toe tap.`,`I could still hear the song.`],mistakes:[`A complicated cup game. Too soon.`],challenge:`Invent a four-beat body pattern and teach your row.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Body percussion.`,items:[{id:`sounds`,kind:`text`,label:`Four body sounds we used:`,lines:2},{id:`vol`,kind:`choice`,label:`While we play we should still`,choices:[`hear the singers`,`cover the singers`,`stop the song`]}]}},{n:26,unit:`form`,short:`Instruments`,title:`Classroom Instruments with Care`,focus:`Rhythm sticks, shakers, bells, drums. How we hold them, how we rest them, how we play a beat.`,outcome:`Students play a steady beat using rhythm instruments and explore their sounds.`,materials:[`Whatever you have: sticks, shakers, triangles, hand drums`,`Rest position taught on day one of instruments`],warmup:{title:`Rest position`,detail:`Instruments on the floor or on shoulders, not making sound. Practice pick up, play four beats, rest. That loop is the whole classroom management system.`},teach:[{title:`One job today: the beat`,detail:`Not the words. Even sounds with the drum or with me.`},{title:`How each one speaks`,detail:`Sticks tap. Shakers shake once per beat (not a sandstorm). Bells ding and wait. Drums are open palm, not fists, unless it is a proper hand drum technique you teach.`},{title:`Quiet is a skill`,detail:`The best players can play so the song is still the star.`},{title:`Passing out and in`,detail:`You pass out. They do not help themselves. Collect the same way. Build this ritual now.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Zin! Zin! Zin! A Violin`,author:`Lloyd Moss (illus. Marjorie Priceman)`,why:`Introduces orchestra instruments with joyful respect—lead-in to careful instrument handling.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Zin-Violin-Lloyd-Moss/dp/0689835248`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach (about 6 min). The story comes before the extra footage in the Reading Rainbow clip.", "before": ["“One instrument joins at a time. Count them with your fingers.”", "“Watch how real musicians hold their instruments and wait.”"], "during": ["Hold up a finger for each new player: trombone solo, trumpet duo, French horn trio, cello quartet, violin quintet, flute, clarinet, oboe, bassoon, then the harp makes ten.", "Mime holding each instrument gently (a slide for trombone, a bow for violin). Then rest hands in laps.", "When the orchestra comes on stage and waits to begin, everyone sits in instrument “rest position.”", "At the end, the audience shouts “Encore!” Talk about how careful players get asked back."], "after": "Pick up, play four beats, rest. Pass out classroom instruments with the book’s rule: play together, then rest together. Move through stations.", "check": "“What is rest position? Why do musicians wait before they play?”", "adapt": "Show photos of the book’s instruments. Children who worry about instruments can be the conductor first.", "extend": "Sort the book’s instruments by how they make sound: blow, bow, or pluck."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jw1HHGacCv0", "channel": "Reading Rainbow (official)"}},activity:{title:`Rotate colours`,detail:`If you have four types, four stations, two minutes each, beat only, then rest. A song underneath the whole time.`},close:`Collect. Count. Thank the instruments. Stamp if rest position worked.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Rest position`,detail:`No sound until the start hand.`},{title:`Play the beat`,detail:`Even. With the teacher.`},{title:`Treat each instrument the way it wants`,detail:`Shake, tap, or ding.`},{title:`Hear the song`,detail:`Play under it, not over it.`},{title:`Give it back with two hands`,detail:`Wait to be asked.`}],teacher:[`Fewer instruments is better than a riot. Half the class can body-perc and switch.`,`Triangles need a fat yarn handle and a rule: one ding per beat.`],checklist:[`I used rest position.`,`I played an even beat.`,`I gave the instrument back with care.`],mistakes:[`Passing out before teaching rest. Always rest first.`],challenge:`Play the beat with eyes on the conductor, not on the instrument.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Instruments.`,items:[{id:`used`,kind:`text`,label:`An instrument I played:`,lines:1},{id:`rest`,kind:`choice`,label:`Rest position means`,choices:[`no sound until the start`,`practice as loud as I want`,`hide the instrument`]}]},studio:`quiz`},{n:27,unit:`form`,short:`Name sound`,title:`Name, Sound, Picture`,focus:`Match an instrument’s name, its sound, and what it looks like. Alberta Grade 1 loves this game.`,outcome:`Students explore the sound of various instruments and match names, sounds, and pictures.`,materials:[`3–6 real instruments or pictures`,`Studio quiz on this site`,`A cloth to hide one instrument`],warmup:{title:`Mystery bag`,detail:`Hide one instrument. Play it unseen. Students guess the name. Reveal.`},teach:[{title:`Three cards for each`,detail:`Word, picture, sound. We connect all three. Start with drum, shaker, bell, sticks.`},{title:`How it makes the sound`,detail:`Hit, shake, scrape. Let them name the action.`},{title:`High or low colour`,detail:`Bell vs drum. Link to week 19 without a lecture.`},{title:`Use the studio`,detail:`The website can play drum, shaker, bell, stick if you have no kit today.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`My Family Plays Music`,author:`Judy Cox (illus. Elbrite Brown)`,why:`Many instruments named and pictured—supports name/sound/picture instrument ID.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Family-Plays-Music/dp/0823440397`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 7 min). Have the real instruments or picture cards ready.", "before": ["“The girl in this book plays with everyone in her family. Listen for her instrument on each page.”", "“Is it hit, shaken, or scraped?”"], "during": ["When she names her instrument, hold up the real one (or the picture). Play it four times on the beat: tambourine with Mom, triangle with Dad, cymbals with her sister, cowbell with her brother, wood block with her aunt, maracas with her uncle, rhythm sticks with Grandpa.", "The class names each one and shows the action: hit, shake, or scrape.", "Point out the family’s big instruments too (fiddle, cello, clarinet, guitar, tuba). Their pictures help with the name game.", "At the end she says she’s a percussionist. Talk about what that word means."], "after": "Match the table: hear an instrument, then walk to its picture card, or use the site quiz in pairs.", "check": "Play four sounds behind a screen. Students name each one.", "adapt": "Put the word under each picture card. English learners can point to answer.", "extend": "Children list a family member’s favourite music and pick a percussion instrument to play along."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oDIKQksaLio", "channel": "Grandma “B” Reads with Me"}},activity:{title:`Match the table`,detail:`Stations: hear, then walk to the picture card. Or a paper match on the worksheet. Then the site quiz in pairs if devices exist — otherwise teacher-led on the projector.`},close:`Four sounds, four names, fast. Stamp if most matches are right.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Guess the mystery sound`,detail:`Listen before you shout the name.`},{title:`Say how it is played`,detail:`Hit, shake, or scrape.`},{title:`Match picture to sound`,detail:`Walk or point.`},{title:`Try the studio buttons`,detail:`If we use the website.`},{title:`Use the real name`,detail:`Drum, shaker, bell, sticks.`}],teacher:[`Include at least one instrument they have not played yet so listening stays sharp.`,`If you have a guest from a local ensemble, this is a good week — but the lesson stands without one.`],checklist:[`I matched a sound to a name.`,`I matched a picture to an instrument.`,`I can tell hit, shake, or scrape.`],mistakes:[`Ten instruments. Four is a Grade 1 set.`],challenge:`Be the mystery player. Class guesses with eyes closed.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Name, sound, picture.`,items:[{id:`hit`,kind:`choice`,label:`A drum is usually`,choices:[`hit`,`blown like a flute`,`plugged in`]},{id:`names`,kind:`text`,label:`Instruments we named today:`,lines:2}]},studio:`quiz`},{n:28,unit:`form`,short:`Found sounds`,title:`Found Sounds and New Ways`,focus:`Music can come from pencils, paper, chairs (gently), and invented instruments. We explore with rules.`,outcome:`Students explore environmental and found sounds and play them with care.`,materials:[`Paper, pencils, a metal water bottle, a box`,`A “no” list: bodies, other people’s things, anything that can break`],warmup:{title:`Paper orchestra`,detail:`Each child has one paper. Explore: tap, wave, rip a tiny corner if allowed. Freeze. Which sound was your favourite?`},teach:[{title:`Found sound has rules`,detail:`We do not wreck school things. We do not use friends as drums. We try, we share, we put it back.`},{title:`Same object, new way`,detail:`A pencil can tap, scrape, or roll. That is “new ways” — Alberta language, kid-sized.`},{title:`Build a tiny piece`,detail:`Four beats of found sound as an ostinato under a known chant.`},{title:`Invented instrument`,detail:`Two objects together (bottle + pencil). Name it. Play a beat. Bow.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Max Found Two Sticks`,author:`Brian Pinkney`,why:`Max turns found sticks and household objects into drums—invented/found instruments.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Found-Sticks-Brian-Pinkney/dp/068981593X`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach (about 6 min). The class knows this book from week 12.", "before": ["“Max makes music from things that aren’t instruments. Listen for each object.”", "“Found sounds have rules: we don’t wreck things, and we don’t use friends as drums.”"], "during": ["Before each page, ask “What will Max drum on next?” Then read: his thighs, Grandpa’s bucket, the hatboxes, the soda bottles, the garbage cans.", "For each one, a child tries a safe classroom version (paper, an empty box, a plastic cup, a pencil on a book) and plays four beats.", "Try one object new ways: tap, scrape, roll. Link it to “same object, new way.”", "Keep a steady beat under the whole reading so the objects become an ostinato."], "after": "Composer pairs: pick two found sounds, write a four-beat idea with icons, and perform it once.", "check": "“What makes something a found sound? What’s one rule for using it?”", "adapt": "Give each child a sound card to hold up. Children who need movement can be “object collectors” and put things back.", "extend": "Invent an instrument from two objects. Name it and play Max’s bucket rhythm on it."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EoP8RXcZWQg", "channel": "The Cleveland Orchestra"}},activity:{title:`Composer pairs`,detail:`Pairs pick two found sounds, write a four-beat idea (icons), perform once.`},close:`Reset the room better than we found it. That is still musicianship. Stamp if exploration stayed inside the rules.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Explore paper`,detail:`Find two different sounds.`},{title:`Hear the rules again`,detail:`Kind to things and people.`},{title:`Invent a pair sound`,detail:`Give it a name.`},{title:`Play four beats under a chant`,detail:`Quiet enough to hear words.`},{title:`Put everything back`,detail:`Musicians tidy.`}],teacher:[`If admin is nervous, skip rips and only tap.`,`This week makes a good photo for families if your school allows.`],checklist:[`I found two sounds from one object.`,`I followed the care rules.`,`I helped tidy.`],mistakes:[`Dumping a junk box with no rules. Rules first, then wonder.`],challenge:`Your invented instrument plays only on the rests of a class pattern.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Found sounds.`,items:[{id:`object`,kind:`text`,label:`I made a sound with:`,lines:1},{id:`way`,kind:`text`,label:`A new way I played it:`,lines:1},{id:`rule`,kind:`choice`,label:`Found sounds must be`,choices:[`safe for people and things`,`as loud as recess`,`taken home without asking`]}]}},{n:29,unit:`form`,short:`Fast slow`,title:`Fast and Slow, Loud and Quiet`,focus:`Tempo and dynamics. We change the music on purpose and still stay together.`,outcome:`Students show loud/quiet and fast/slow and follow a conductor for both.`,materials:[`Known song`,`Cue cards: fast / slow / loud / quiet`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Follow my feet`,detail:`Walk slow, walk faster, freeze. Then clap piano (quiet) and forte (loud) — use the kid words plus the music words once.`},teach:[{title:`Two pairs`,detail:`Fast/slow is speed. Loud/quiet is volume. Do not mix the cards at first.`},{title:`The song stays the same`,detail:`Rain, Rain slow. Rain, Rain faster. Same tune. Then quiet vs strong. Same tune.`},{title:`Conductor tools`,detail:`Small hand = quiet. Big hand = stronger. Slow scoop vs quick tick.`},{title:`Piano and forte`,detail:`Say the words once. They do not need to spell them. Quiet and loud remain on the cards.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Trombone Shorty`,author:`Troy Andrews (illus. Bryan Collier)`,why:`Energetic New Orleans music story supports talking about tempo and dynamics in performance.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Trombone-Shorty-Troy-Andrews/dp/1419714651`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 7 min). It’s a longer read, so show key pages and summarise the middle.", "before": ["“Listen for fast music and slow music, loud music and quiet music in Troy’s story.”", "Hold up the four cards: fast, slow, loud, quiet."], "during": ["Every time you read “Where y’at?” the class echoes it. Use the cards to change it: quiet, loud, slow, fast.", "On the Mardi Gras parade pages, march in place with a faster beat. For Troy practising alone at night, use a slow, quiet beat.", "When he picks up a broken trombone twice his size, do slow arm slides for the trombone. Then pick up the tempo as the parade passes.", "When he plays along with Bo Diddley, the class follows your conductor hand: big for loud, small for quiet."], "after": "Four-card song: a student shows a card and the class sings Rain, Rain that way. Rotate helpers.", "check": "“Fast and slow is about ___? Loud and quiet is about ___?” (Speed. Volume.)", "adapt": "Keep only two cards at first (fast/slow). English learners can match arm size and speed without the words.", "extend": "Listen to 20 seconds of a New Orleans brass band. Is it fast or slow? Loud or quiet?"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QitquEL48Co", "channel": "Storyline Online", "label": "Watch Angela Bassett read it"}},activity:{title:`Four-card song`,detail:`A student helper shows a card. Class sings the known song that way. Rotate helpers.`},close:`One child conducts quiet to loud on a long loo. Stamp if the class actually changes.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Walk slow, then faster`,detail:`Still a beat.`},{title:`Clap quiet, then stronger`,detail:`Not a scream.`},{title:`Sing the song four ways`,detail:`Watch the card.`},{title:`Name piano and forte`,detail:`Quiet and loud.`},{title:`Conduct one change`,detail:`If you are the helper, show the card early.`}],teacher:[`Faster + louder often glue together. Pull them apart with separate rounds.`,`Never ask Grade 1 for fortissimo. Strong and kind is the ceiling.`],checklist:[`I can sing slow and faster.`,`I can sing quiet and stronger.`,`I watched the conductor.`],mistakes:[`Faster meaning sloppy. Keep the words clear.`],challenge:`Change in the middle of the song, not just at the start.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Expression.`,items:[{id:`tempo`,kind:`choice`,label:`Fast and slow are about`,choices:[`speed`,`how high the notes are`,`the instrument’s name`]},{id:`dyn`,kind:`choice`,label:`Piano means`,choices:[`quiet`,`the loudest possible`,`stop`]}]}},{n:30,unit:`form`,short:`Listen well`,title:`Listen with Respect`,focus:`We listen to music of First Nations, Métis, and Inuit peoples, and music from many places, with care. We do not copy what is not ours to copy.`,outcome:`Students listen with purpose to music of diverse cultures, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit music, and practise respectful audience skills.`,materials:[`One short recording you have rights to play (school library, licensed service, or a visiting artist)`,`A map of Turtle Island / Canada`,`Listening map paper`],warmup:{title:`How we sit to listen`,detail:`Still bodies, looking at the speaker or the player. One listen with eyes. Name what we will listen for: drum, voice, fiddle, or dance feet.`},teach:[{title:`Respect first`,detail:`Some songs are sacred or family-owned. In this class we listen. We do not pretend, costume, or make up “Indigenous-sounding” chants. If a local Nation or Métis artist has shared a song for schools, we credit them by name.`},{title:`One focus listen`,detail:`Play 45–90 seconds. Ask: drum or fiddle? Fast or slow? For dancing or for sitting? Accept kid words.`},{title:`Métis fiddle and jig is one good door`,detail:`If you use a jig, students may pat a beat or try a simple step only if you are teaching a school-appropriate dance, not a mockery. Movement is optional. Listening is the job.`},{title:`Many cultures`,detail:`If time, a second short piece from another place the class has a real link to (a classmate’s family, a book you read). Same respectful frame.`}],story:{title:`The listening chair`,body:`Sam wanted to bang along the first time the drum came through the speaker. Ms. Cole lifted the quiet hand. “This drum already has players,” she said. “Today we borrow our ears, not their song.” Sam sat on his hands and heard the pattern he had missed. After, he said the drum was like walking. That was enough.`},storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`We Belong to the Drum`,author:`Sandra Lamouche (illus. Azby Whitecalf)`,why:`Cree-authored story about the drum, belonging, and culturally safe listening—not a sacred-song how-to.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/We-Belong-Drum-Sandra-Lamouche/dp/1459834356`,careNote:`Cultural care: listen and appreciate only; do not teach or copy sacred songs.`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach, sitting in listening posture (about 7 min). Read it as a story to listen to. It isn’t a song to copy.", "before": ["“Nikosis heard the drum before he was born, in his mother’s heartbeat. Put a hand on your heart. Can you feel yours?”", "“Today our job is to listen and to care.”"], "during": ["Read it straight through as a listening story. No drumming along and no pretend powwow singing.", "Stop once when Nikosis feels scared at daycare. “How do you think he feels? What might help?”", "When the teacher plays powwow music and he feels he belongs, ask: “How did the music help him?”", "Show the Plains Cree words in the book, and credit the author, Sandra Lamouche, and the illustrator, Azby Whitecalf."], "after": "Play a short recording shared by a named artist, then Draw what you heard (dots for drum, lines for voice). Share one noticing with a partner.", "check": "“What does a respectful listener look and sound like?”", "adapt": "Show the pictures slowly and let children point to what they notice. Some children may connect to their own family music. Welcome that without asking them to perform.", "extend": "Invite a local Indigenous musician or Elder through your school’s protocols, and follow their lead."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GKqZYOC8NmA", "channel": "Baker & Taylor", "label": "Watch the author read the opening (sample)"}},activity:{title:`Draw what you heard`,detail:`A listening map: dots for drum, line for voice or fiddle. Share one noticing with a partner. No performing of the recording.`},close:`Credit the artists again. “Thank you for the listen.” Stamp if the room stayed in listening mode.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sit like an audience`,detail:`Still. Ready ears.`},{title:`Know the rule`,detail:`We listen. We do not copy sacred or family songs.`},{title:`Listen once for a job`,detail:`Drum? Voice? Fast or slow?`},{title:`Draw a listening map`,detail:`Simple.`},{title:`Say one noticing`,detail:`Kind words. Credit the musicians.`}],teacher:[`Do not use random uncredited clips. Name the performer and Nation or community if it is known and appropriate.`,`If you are not sure a song is okay to teach, do not teach it. Listening with credit is the safe, good Grade 1 move.`,`Invite a local artist through proper school channels if you can — even a 15-minute visit beats a shaky recording.`],checklist:[`I sat and listened.`,`I can tell one thing I heard.`,`I know we do not copy songs that are not ours to sing.`],mistakes:[`Making feather headbands or fake drums. Never.`,`Calling all First Nations music “powwow.” Be specific or stay general with respect.`],challenge:`Write a thank-you sentence to the musicians we heard.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Listening with respect.`,items:[{id:`heard`,kind:`text`,label:`I heard:`,lines:2},{id:`rule`,kind:`choice`,label:`In this lesson we`,choices:[`listen and credit the musicians`,`make up a pretend sacred song`,`talk over the recording`]}]},faith:`Optional: listening without grabbing is a way to honour our neighbours.`},{n:31,unit:`concert`,short:`Same different`,title:`Same and Different`,focus:`Form we can feel: this part is the same, that part is different. Echo and verse live here.`,outcome:`Students identify same and different phrases and simple call-and-response form.`,materials:[`Two coloured scarves or cards: SAME / DIFFERENT`,`A song with two phrases`],warmup:{title:`Copy or change`,detail:`Teacher claps a pattern. Second time is either identical or one beat different. Thumbs.`},teach:[{title:`Phrases are musical sentences`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain phrase 1, phrase 2. Are they the same? Mostly yes with a small tail. Keep it honest.`},{title:`A song with a chorus`,detail:`If you have a simple verse/chorus (even a hello song with a repeated line), hold SAME on the repeat.`},{title:`Call and response is a form`,detail:`Teacher call, class response. That is not just a trick — it is a shape. Name it.`},{title:`Map it with colour`,detail:`Blue card each time the same phrase returns. Yellow for a new one.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`We're Going on a Bear Hunt`,author:`Michael Rosen (illus. Helen Oxenbury)`,why:`Same refrain returns after different obstacle verses—clear same/different musical form.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Were-Going-Bear-Michael-Rosen/dp/0689853491`,guide:{"when": "Brain break before Card holders (about 6 min). The class knows this from week 5.", "before": ["“Listen for the part that is the same every time and the part that changes.”", "Hold up a blue SAME card and a yellow DIFFERENT card."], "during": ["Every time the chorus returns (“We’re going on a bear hunt…” and “We can’t go over it, we can’t go under it…”), a helper holds up SAME.", "For each new place (the grass, the river, the mud, the forest, the snowstorm, the cave), a helper holds up DIFFERENT. The sound words change every time.", "Map it on the board with coloured squares: blue, yellow, blue, yellow… Children see the pattern.", "The run home goes through the places backwards. Ask: “Same or different from before?”"], "after": "Card holders: two students hold SAME and DIFFERENT while the class sings a known song. Cards pop up. Switch helpers.", "check": "“Which part of the book was the same each time? Which part changed?”", "adapt": "Use colours only, not words, for English learners. Movers can stand up on SAME and sit on DIFFERENT.", "extend": "Write a class verse: same chorus, new place, new sound word."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0gyI6ykDwds", "channel": "Walker Books", "label": "Watch Michael Rosen perform it"}},activity:{title:`Card holders`,detail:`Two students hold SAME and DIFFERENT. Class sings. Cards pop up. Switch helpers.`},close:`Ask for a song that feels like echo (same) vs a song that tells two different ideas. Stamp if cards match the music.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Thumbs for copy or change`,detail:`Listen to claps.`},{title:`Sing a song in two phrases`,detail:`Are they the same?`},{title:`Hold a form card`,detail:`Up at the right time.`},{title:`Be the call or the response`,detail:`Wait for your job.`},{title:`Name the shape`,detail:`Same, different, or echo.`}],teacher:[`ABA can wait. Same/different is the Grade 1 form win.`,`Charlie Over the Ocean is a strong call-response if you need a new song.`],checklist:[`I can tell same from different.`,`I know echo is a kind of same.`,`I helped show form with a card.`],mistakes:[`Graphing five sections. Two is enough.`],challenge:`Make a call. The class must respond with something different on purpose.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Same or different?`,items:[{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`Echo is usually`,choices:[`the same as the leader`,`always a brand new song`,`silence`]},{id:`shape`,kind:`text`,label:`A song we mapped today:`,lines:1}]}},{n:32,unit:`concert`,short:`Call back`,title:`Call and Response`,focus:`A leader sings, the group answers. We practise being both.`,outcome:`Students perform call and response with accurate waiting, matching, and kind leadership.`,materials:[`Charlie Over the Ocean or a hello call`,`Circle`],warmup:{title:`Name call`,detail:`Teacher: “Charlie over the ocean.” Class: echo. Then a child’s name in the call if the song allows. Keep it short.`},teach:[{title:`Jobs`,detail:`Leader waits after the call. Group does not steal the call. If you are not the leader, you are the response — that is a real job.`},{title:`Teach the song`,detail:`Charlie Over the Ocean (or Kye Kye Kule). Echo by phrase, then call-response in two groups, then with a student leader.`},{title:`Matching pitch still matters`,detail:`The response should sound like the call’s cousin, not a yell.`},{title:`Kind leaders`,detail:`The leader faces the group, uses a singing voice, and thanks the group after.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Polar Bear, Polar Bear, What Do You Hear?`,author:`Bill Martin Jr. (illus. Eric Carle)`,why:`Question–answer animal sounds are a natural call-and-response pattern for Grade 1.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Polar-Bear-What-Do-You-Hear/dp/0805053883`,guide:{"when": "Hook for Teach (about 5 min). The question-and-answer pattern is the lesson.", "before": ["“Every page has a question and an answer. The question is the call and the answer is the response.”", "“The response waits for the call to finish.”"], "during": ["Teacher sings the call on so–mi (“Polar Bear, Polar Bear, what do you hear?”). The class chants the response (“I hear a lion roaring in my ear”).", "Give each animal sound its own small colour: hippo snorting (low voices), flamingo fluting (high “oo”), boa hissing (a whisper), elephant trumpeting, walrus bellowing.", "Switch jobs: a student leader sings the call and the class responds.", "At the end, the children make all the animal sounds. Hold a quiet hand to stop the chaos. That’s part of the job."], "after": "Charlie Over the Ocean with many leaders. Use the book’s rule: wait for the call to finish.", "check": "“What’s the leader’s job? What’s the group’s job?” (Call, then response.)", "adapt": "The repeated pattern works well for English learners. Offer animal picture cards to shy leaders.", "extend": "Make a class version with instruments: “Drum, drum, what do you hear? I hear a shaker shaking in my ear.”"},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tMACKTLvGDc", "channel": "The World of Eric Carle"}},activity:{title:`Many leaders`,detail:`As many student calls as time allows. Sitting leaders welcome.`},close:`Group response without a reminder. Stamp if waiting is clean.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Learn the call`,detail:`Echo the teacher.`},{title:`Be the group`,detail:`Wait. Then answer.`},{title:`Lead if you want`,detail:`Singing voice. Face your friends.`},{title:`Match, don’t shout`,detail:`The answer is music.`},{title:`Thank the group`,detail:`Leaders are polite.`}],teacher:[`Skip ocean if water language is a classroom issue; use a hello call instead.`,`Kye Kye Kule: credit it as a Ghanaian game song commonly shared in schools, teach respectfully, keep the game gentle.`],checklist:[`I waited for my job.`,`I used a singing voice in the response.`,`I can be a leader or a group member.`],mistakes:[`Letting 24 children run a chasing game. Sit to sing, then a small movement if any.`],challenge:`Invent a two-beat call on so–mi. Class answers so–mi–so.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Call and response.`,items:[{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`If I am not the leader I`,choices:[`sing the response`,`talk`,`start a new game`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`Our call-and-response song:`,lines:1}]}},{n:33,unit:`concert`,short:`Choose`,title:`Choose What We Will Share`,focus:`Pick two songs and one played piece for the sharing concert. Rehearse the order.`,outcome:`Students help select repertoire and practise a simple concert order.`,materials:[`List of known songs`,`The four-beat patterns / little melodies from earlier projects`,`Board: 1, 2, 3`],warmup:{title:`Memory lane`,detail:`Hum four known songs. Students raise hands for the ones they feel ready to share.`},teach:[{title:`What makes a good share song`,detail:`We know the words. We can start and stop. We still like it. Not the newest, hardest thing.`},{title:`Vote with kindness`,detail:`We may not pick everyone’s first choice. We pick what this class can do well together.`},{title:`The played piece`,detail:`Body percussion ostinato, a found-sound pattern, or a student four-beat echoed by all. One only.`},{title:`Write the order`,detail:`Song, played piece, song. Practise walking to standing spots between them. That transition is the concert.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Pete the Cat: I Love My White Shoes`,author:`Eric Litwin (illus. James Dean)`,why:`Class favorite song is a natural choice when selecting repertoire to share at a concert.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Pete-Cat-Love-White-Shoes/dp/0061906220`,guide:{"when": "Warm-up or Memory lane (about 5 min). Use it as one candidate song for the concert.", "before": ["“A good share song: we know it, we can start and stop, and we still like it. Is Pete’s song one?”", "Hand in (start) and hand out (stop)."], "during": ["Sing through it once with a student conductor starting and stopping each colour verse.", "Practise a clean ending: after the last verse (wet shoes), everyone stops on the conductor’s closed hand and freezes.", "Try the transition: sit for the story parts, stand for Pete’s song. Transitions are part of the concert.", "Vote kindly: thumbs up if you feel ready to share it."], "after": "Draft run: put the chosen order on the board (song, played piece, song) and time one full run.", "check": "“Why is this song a good choice (or not) for our concert?”", "adapt": "A child who is nervous about singing can hold the colour cards on stage.", "extend": "Add a class-made verse to perform as a surprise ending."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QNAeRy6jS7c", "channel": "HarperKids", "label": "Watch read & sing-along"}},activity:{title:`Draft run`,detail:`Stand, piece 1, sit or stay, piece 2, piece 3, bow (a small nod). Time it. Aim under five minutes.`},close:`Write the program on the board and leave it up. Stamp if the order survived one full run.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Name a song you feel ready for`,detail:`Honest.`},{title:`Accept the class choices`,detail:`Kind even if yours was not picked.`},{title:`Learn the order`,detail:`1, 2, 3.`},{title:`Practise the standing spots`,detail:`Quiet feet.`},{title:`Nod at the end`,detail:`That is our bow.`}],teacher:[`You may override a vote if the favourite song is still a shout. You are the musician in the room.`,`Invite a buddy class or families for week 35 now.`],checklist:[`I know our three pieces.`,`I know the order.`,`I practised a small bow.`],mistakes:[`Adding a fourth piece. Three is plenty.`],challenge:`Be the announcer: say the name of piece 1 in a talking voice.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our program.`,items:[{id:`p1`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 1:`,lines:1},{id:`p2`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 2:`,lines:1},{id:`p3`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 3:`,lines:1}]}},{n:34,unit:`concert`,short:`Audience`,title:`How to Be an Audience`,focus:`When we are not the ones singing, we still have a job: still, kind, clap at the end.`,outcome:`Students demonstrate appropriate audience behaviour and follow a conductor as performers.`,materials:[`A short audio or a teacher solo`,`Practice chairs as “audience”`],warmup:{title:`Bad audience / good audience`,detail:`Teacher models wiggling and talking. Students freeze it. Then model still and watching. Name the difference. Keep it funny, not shaming.`},teach:[{title:`Audience rules`,detail:`Voices off. Bodies on the chair. Eyes on the players. Clap when the piece is done, not in the rest in the middle. No phones — say it if families will attend.`},{title:`Performer rules`,detail:`Wait for the start. If you mess up, keep going. Smile at the end.`},{title:`Rehearse both jobs`,detail:`Half class performs piece 1, half is audience. Swap.`},{title:`The clap`,detail:`Two or three seconds of clapping, then rest. Not a stomp-clap chant unless you choose that as a class.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Never Play Music Right Next to the Zoo`,author:`John Lithgow (illus. Leeza Hernandez)`,why:`Concert-going story with the punchline: pay attention when an orchestra plays.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Never-Play-Music-Right-Next/dp/1442467436`,guide:{"when": "Hook at the start of Teach (about 7 min). Lithgow’s reading on Storyline Online fits well.", "before": ["“The boy in this story is at a concert. Listen for what goes wrong for him as an audience member.”", "Model a good listener: still body, eyes on the players."], "during": ["The chorus returns: “Never play music right next to the zoo.” The class says it with you each time.", "When the animals take over (monkeys on fiddle, bison on bass, the yak on sax, the hippo on tuba), pretend-play each instrument for four beats. Then freeze on your quiet hand.", "Notice that the audience stays calm and keeps watching. Ask: “What does a good audience do when something surprising happens?”", "End on rule number two: don’t fall asleep when an orchestra plays."], "after": "Rehearse both jobs: half the class performs a concert piece, half is the audience. Swap. Practise a two-second clap at the end.", "check": "“Name one audience rule and one performer rule.”", "adapt": "Show a picture of “audience” (chairs, eyes on the stage). Children who need movement can play the animal parts in the retelling.", "extend": "Draw your favourite animal musician and write (or tell) what instrument it plays."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1WyBEQ5Od2M", "channel": "Storyline Online", "label": "Watch John Lithgow read it"}},activity:{title:`Dress rehearsal without costumes`,detail:`Full order. Real start and stop. Teacher only conducts. No extra teaching talk if you can help it.`},close:`One compliment from audience to performers, one from performers to audience. Stamp if both jobs appeared.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Practise still sitting`,detail:`Audience job.`},{title:`Clap at the end only`,detail:`Wait for the last sound.`},{title:`Perform your piece`,detail:`Keep going if you wobble.`},{title:`Swap jobs`,detail:`Both are music.`},{title:`Full order once`,detail:`Like the real share.`}],teacher:[`If families come next week, send the family letter today.`,`Plan where coats and siblings go. Grade 1 concerts fail in the hallway, not on the songs.`],checklist:[`I can be a still audience.`,`I clap at the end.`,`I kept going as a performer.`],mistakes:[`A lecture on etiquette with no practice. Do the jobs.`],challenge:`Be the person who starts the clap when the conductor’s hands drop.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Audience and performer.`,items:[{id:`aud`,kind:`choice`,label:`An audience`,choices:[`watches and claps at the end`,`sings the whole time from their seat`,`walks around`]},{id:`perf`,kind:`choice`,label:`If I make a mistake I`,choices:[`keep going`,`stop the concert`,`laugh at my friend`]}]}},{n:35,unit:`concert`,short:`Share`,title:`Sharing Concert`,focus:`Hand-in project 4. We share two songs and one played piece with our audience.`,outcome:`Students sing in unison with growing expression and perform for an audience with confidence.`,materials:[`Program on the board`,`Spots marked`,`Audience (buddy class, families, or stuffed animals if needed)`],warmup:{title:`Body and breath`,detail:`Shake out. Tall. One so–mi on loo. Quiet hand. That is the whole warmup — do not tire them.`},teach:[{title:`This is the share, not a new lesson`,detail:`No new songs. Remind order. Remind still feet. Remind smile.`},{title:`If the audience is late`,detail:`Sing the warmup song sitting. Do not lose the room.`},{title:`You run it`,detail:`Announcer (teacher or a child) names piece 1. Conduct. Nod. Piece 2. Piece 3. Thank the audience.`},{title:`After`,detail:`Sit. Two comments from you that are specific (“Your rest in the rhythm piece was real silence”).`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Zin! Zin! Zin! A Violin`,author:`Lloyd Moss (illus. Marjorie Priceman)`,why:`Orchestra gathers and the audience shouts “Encore!”—perfect sharing-concert celebration.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Zin-Violin-Lloyd-Moss/dp/0689835248`,guide:{"when": "Before the concert as a calm-down, or after the concert as a celebration (about 6 min).", "before": ["“We’re going to count the musicians as they join, like our class joining the concert.”", "“What do audiences shout at the end if they want more?” (Encore!)"], "during": ["Count instruments on fingers as they arrive (solo, duo, trio… up to ten). Talk about how our songs came together one step at a time.", "When the orchestra walks on stage and waits, the class practises concert posture: tall, still, eyes on the conductor.", "When the music soars, the class sways gently. Take one deep breath together.", "At the ending, practise a real audience clap, then a quiet “encore” whisper."], "after": "Run the concert (Project 4). Afterwards, sit and give two specific compliments, like the book’s audience.", "check": "“What did you do today that real musicians do?”", "adapt": "Keep the reading short if nerves are high: just the counting pages. Let anxious children hold the book.", "extend": "Children draw their class as the orchestra, with a label for each group’s part."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jw1HHGacCv0", "channel": "Reading Rainbow (official)"}},activity:{title:`Project 4 — share`,detail:`Do the program. If no visitors, invite another class or perform for the principal on a walk-by. Stuffed animal audience is a real backup and still counts.`},close:`Thank the musicians. Stamp project 4. Take a still photo if allowed.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stand on your spot`,detail:`Quiet feet.`},{title:`Watch the start`,detail:`Breathe with the teacher.`},{title:`Sing or play our three pieces`,detail:`Keep going.`},{title:`Nod at the end`,detail:`Then sit for the thank-you.`},{title:`Be a kind audience if others play`,detail:`Still bodies.`}],teacher:[`Have water and a tissue. Have a second adult if families come.`,`Rubric: unison, a bit of expression, audience manners. Do not dock a child for a wobble.`],checklist:[`I stood on my spot.`,`I watched the teacher.`,`I shared our music.`],mistakes:[`Stopping to reteach mid-concert. Keep going.`],challenge:`Help a friend find their spot without talking.`,worksheet:{prompt:`After our share.`,items:[{id:`felt`,kind:`text`,label:`I felt:`,lines:2},{id:`proud`,kind:`text`,label:`I am proud that we:`,lines:2}]}},{n:36,unit:`concert`,short:`What I know`,title:`What I Know About Music Now`,focus:`Remember the year: beat, ta and ti-ti, so–mi–la, instruments, listening with respect. Celebrate.`,outcome:`Students reflect on singing, playing, moving, and listening growth from the year.`,materials:[`A few favourite songs`,`Worksheets from the year if you kept them`,`A simple celebration (stickers are optional, singing is the treat)`],warmup:{title:`Medley of us`,detail:`Thirty seconds each of four favourite songs. No teaching. Just the joy.`},teach:[{title:`Name the big ideas`,detail:`Beat. Long and short. High and low. Kind listening. Unison. Let students finish your sentences.`},{title:`Show what you know`,detail:`Stations if you can: clap a four-beat card, sign so–mi, play a beat on a shaker, sit as audience for a 20-second listen.`},{title:`A letter to next year’s class`,detail:`One sentence: “In Grade 1 music you will…” Teacher can scribe for those who need it.`},{title:`Thank you`,detail:`Thank their voices. Thank their waiting. Thank their families if that is your culture.`}],storySuggestion:{bookTitle:`Because`,author:`Mo Willems (illus. Amber Ren)`,why:`Chain of musical moments leads a child to the stage—celebrates a year of music learning.`,linkType:`amazon`,url:`https://www.amazon.ca/Because-Mo-Willems/dp/1368019013`,guide:{"when": "Close of the year: read it before the portfolio page, or as the final 5 minutes before the week 1 hello song (about 6 min).", "before": ["“This book is a chain of ‘becauses.’ Listen for how one small thing leads to music.”", "“What’s one ‘because’ from our year?”"], "during": ["Each time a page starts with “Because…,” the class taps one soft beat. That makes a steady chain.", "When the girl sits in row C, seat 14 and hears the orchestra, pause. Everyone closes their eyes for five seconds of listening.", "When she practises and starts to write music, connect it to our own four-beat patterns and so–mi–la tunes.", "At the end, when her music is played at the concert hall, ask: “Who might your music change someday?”"], "after": "Make a class “because” chain aloud: “Because we learned the beat, we could…” Then sing the week 1 hello song and notice how it feels now.", "check": "“Name one big music idea from this year: beat, long and short, high and low, or kind listening.”", "adapt": "Give sentence starters (“Because I…”) and scribe for children who need it.", "extend": "Children write one “because” sentence for their letter to next year’s Grade 1 class."},readAloud:{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1typJezWdEY", "channel": "Music With Miss C"}},activity:{title:`Portfolio page`,detail:`Worksheet plus one last song standing. Optional: they teach a so–mi hello to a stuffed guest or a Grade 1 buddy from another class.`},close:`Sing the very first hello song from week 1. Notice how it feels now. Stamp the year.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sing a favourite`,detail:`With the class.`},{title:`Show a skill at a station`,detail:`Beat, signs, or listening.`},{title:`Tell what you know`,detail:`One sentence is enough.`},{title:`Write or say a tip for next year`,detail:`Kind and true.`},{title:`Sing hello like week 1`,detail:`Notice your singing voice.`}],teacher:[`This is not a test week. It is a mirror.`,`Send leftover recordings home only with permission.`,`Note two students who grew quietly — tell them out loud.`],checklist:[`I sang a favourite song.`,`I showed one music skill.`,`I said or wrote something I know about music.`],mistakes:[`A paper exam. Grade 1 shows music by doing it.`],challenge:`Teach a grown-up at home the quiet hand and a so–mi hello.`,worksheet:{prompt:`What I know about music now.`,items:[{id:`know`,kind:`text`,label:`I know that music can:`,lines:2},{id:`love`,kind:`text`,label:`A song I love:`,lines:1},{id:`tip`,kind:`text`,label:`A tip for next year’s Grade 1:`,lines:2}]},faith:`Optional: we give thanks for voices, for waiting, and for music we were allowed to share.`}],i=[{id:`voice`,n:1,title:`Voice & Echo`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 1 – 6`,start:1,end:6,blurb:`Speaking voice, singing voice, echo games, and so–mi.`,outcome:`Students distinguish speaking and singing voices and echo simple songs in tune.`},{id:`rhythm`,n:2,title:`Steady Beat`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 7 – 12`,start:7,end:12,blurb:`Feel, walk, and play a steady beat. Strong beats and freeze games.`,outcome:`Students show that music may move to a steady beat, with the body and with instruments.`},{id:`melody`,n:3,title:`Long, Short, Silence`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 13 – 18`,start:13,end:18,blurb:`Ta, ti-ti, and rest. Four-beat patterns a Grade 1 can clap and write with icons.`,outcome:`Students hear long sounds, short sounds, and silences, and clap ta, ti-ti, and rest.`},{id:`timbre`,n:4,title:`High & Low`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 19 – 24`,start:19,end:24,blurb:`Melody goes up and down. So–mi–la hand signs and tiny tunes.`,outcome:`Students show high and low, and sing so–mi and so–mi–la with hand signs.`},{id:`form`,n:5,title:`Instruments & Colour`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 25 – 30`,start:25,end:30,blurb:`Body percussion, classroom instruments, found sounds, loud and quiet, fast and slow.`,outcome:`Students explore instrument sounds, match name–sound–picture, and play a beat with care.`},{id:`concert`,n:6,title:`Songs to Share`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 31 – 36`,start:31,end:36,blurb:`Same and different, call and response, audience manners, and a class sharing.`,outcome:`Students sing in unison with expression and listen as a kind audience.`}],a=[{n:1,title:`Our class song`,due:`Week 6`,week:6,blurb:`The class sings one echo or so–mi song in unison, standing tall, with a start and a stop together.`,must:[`Most voices use a singing voice, not a shout`,`The class starts and stops with the teacher’s hand`,`Students can tell the song’s words to a partner`],rubric:[`Singing voice 2`,`Starting and stopping together 1`,`Knowing the words 1`]},{n:2,title:`Beat parade`,due:`Week 12`,week:12,blurb:`Students walk, pat, or play a steady beat while a known song is sung. Freeze on the silence.`,must:[`Beat stays even (not the rhythm of the words)`,`Freeze on the rest or the teacher’s stop`,`Safe feet and quiet instruments`],rubric:[`Steady beat 2`,`Freeze / control 1`,`Safe, careful playing 1`]},{n:3,title:`Four-beat pattern`,due:`Week 18`,week:18,blurb:`Each student claps or plays one four-beat pattern using ta, ti-ti, and rest, then the class echoes it.`,must:[`Pattern is four beats long`,`Uses at least two of: ta, ti-ti, rest`,`Class can echo it once`],rubric:[`Correct length 1`,`Clear ta / ti-ti / rest 2`,`Echoable for the class 1`]},{n:4,title:`Sharing concert`,due:`Week 35`,week:35,blurb:`The class shares two songs and one rhythm or instrument piece with a small audience (buddy class, families, or just the principal).`,must:[`Two songs sung in unison`,`One played or clapped piece`,`Audience job practiced the week before`],rubric:[`Unison singing 2`,`Expression (loud/quiet or still/moving) 1`,`Audience manners 1`]}],o=[...n,...r];function s(e){return o.find(t=>t.n===e)??o[0]}function c(e){return i.find(t=>t.id===e)??i[0]}function l(e){return o.filter(t=>t.unit===e)}function u(e){return c(s(e).unit)}function d(t){return e[t]??[]}function f(e){return t[e]}var p=[{element:`Rhythm`,items:[`Demonstrate pulse (individual / group).`,`Identify pulse as ta.`,`Differentiate sound and silence; identify silence as rest.`,`Experience and identify fast / slow.`,`Identify ti-ti / ta.`,`Experience meter (2/4, 4/4, 6/8).`]},{element:`Melody`,items:[`Differentiate high / low and experience up / down.`,`Build a folk, seasonal, multicultural, and patriotic repertoire.`,`Experience so–mi and so–mi–la melodies.`,`Identify SM / SML tones and hand signs.`,`Demonstrate contour.`]},{element:`Harmony`,items:[`Develop proper mallet technique.`,`Demonstrate a simple bordun (chord).`,`Experience ostinato.`]},{element:`Form`,items:[`Experience same / different.`,`Identify phrase, introduction, and the repeat sign.`,`Identify same / different as AB (and ABA).`]},{element:`Timbre`,items:[`Identify unpitched percussion.`,`Differentiate vocal qualities: speak, sing, whisper, shout.`,`Demonstrate loud / quiet.`,`Classify unpitched as wood / metal / skin.`,`Identify four levels of body percussion: snap, clap, pat, stamp.`,`Classify pitched percussion as wood / metal.`]}],m=[{element:`Rhythm`,items:[`Music may move to a steady beat.`,`Music may move evenly or unevenly.`,`Music is made up of long sounds, short sounds and silences.`,`There are strong and weak beats in music.`,`Ta, ti-ti, and rest can show those sounds and silences.`]},{element:`Melody`,items:[`Sounds may be high or low.`,`A melody can move up, down, or stay the same.`,`Grade 1 sings so–mi and then so–mi–la, with hand signs.`]},{element:`Expression`,items:[`Music can be loud or quiet, fast or slow.`,`We start and stop together when a conductor’s hand asks us to.`]},{element:`Timbre`,items:[`A speaking voice and a singing voice sound different.`,`Classroom instruments and found sounds have their own colour.`]},{element:`Form`,items:[`Music can be the same or different.`,`Echo, call and response, and verse are forms Grade 1 can feel.`]},{element:`Skills`,items:[`Sing in unison, alone and with the group.`,`Move to the beat: walk, pat, freeze, hop as the body is ready.`,`Play a steady beat on rhythm instruments.`,`Listen with care, including music of First Nations, Métis, Inuit, and many cultures.`]}];export{o as a,c,l as d,i,u as l,m as n,f as o,a as r,d as s,p as t,s as u};